window.SAMPLES = {"neuropsychology":{"title":"Neuropsychology — Master Exam Guide","text":"# 🧩 Neuropsychology — Master Exam Guide\n### Medical Psychology Module (1 CFU) · Prof. Roberta Ferrucci (lecture by S. Oliveri, DISS) · a.a. 2025–2026\n\n> **This guide = the Neuropsychology branch.** Built from the lecture *\"Clinical Neuropsychology & Neuropsychological Assessment in Neurodegenerative Diseases\"* + the 3 readings (Casaletto & Heaton 2017; Schaefer et al. 2024; Harsh et al. 2025).\n> 🔥 **The two examined heavyweights = NEGLECT and the APRAXIAS.** The triple-network model (DMN/SN/CEN) is already in your **PNEI** section.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 How they examine you\n- 🔥 MCQs = **crisp definitions of the named syndromes** — neglect, the apraxia subtypes, the aphasias, agnosia — plus **brain–behaviour localisation** (which lesion → which deficit).\n- 🔥 The two recalled items were **\"right-hemisphere lesion → neglect of the left side\"** and **\"ideomotor vs ideational apraxia.\"** Nail these cold.\n- Inattentional blindness also appears here (shared with General Psychology).\n\n**🔥 = appeared on the 23/06 exam.**\n\n---\n---\n\n# 1️⃣ What is Neuropsychology\n> The branch of psychology studying **how the brain & nervous system influence cognition and behaviour** — i.e. the **brain–behaviour relationship**, and how brain injury/illness affects cognitive functions.\n\n| **Clinical neuropsychology** | **Experimental neuropsychology** |\n|---|---|\n| Studies **brain-injured** patients | Studies **normal** (intact) brains |\n| Measures deficits (intelligence, personality, sensorimotor) via standardised tests | Lab study of higher functions in healthy subjects |\n| Relates test results to **specific affected brain areas** | Records accuracy, response speed, electrophysiology |\n| Aims: **diagnose** lesions + support **rehabilitation** | Aim: **infer brain organisation** |\n\n- **Lesions** can be **circumscribed** (e.g. surgical) or **diffuse** (e.g. degenerative disease).\n- 📚 Related distinction: **Cognitive neuropsychology** = uses lesions to *decipher normal cognition* (e.g. Broca on aphasia); **Clinical neuropsychology** = the *applied* assessment + rehabilitation discipline.\n\n---\n\n# 2️⃣ The cognitive functions map (what gets assessed)\n| Function | Components |\n|---|---|\n| **Orientation** | Personal, spatial, temporal, familial coordinates |\n| **Language** | Verbal fluency, comprehension |\n| **Memory** | Working, short-/long-term verbal, visuospatial, learning, procedural, autobiographical |\n| **Executive / attention / reasoning** | Selective/divided/sustained attention, reaction times, reasoning, mental flexibility, planning, control |\n| **Praxis (apraxia)** | Ideative, bucco-facial, ideomotor, constructive |\n| **Visuospatial / perceptual** | Process visual info, spatial relationships, object location, 3D navigation |\n\n---\n\n# 3️⃣ Perception & Attention (the neuro angle)\n*(Overlaps with General Psychology — kept brief; same Gestalt/transduction logic.)*\n- **Sensation** = passive raw stimulation of sense organs → **Transduction** (physical → neural energy) → **Perception** = active organisation & interpretation (select → re-elaborate → recognise via memory → give meaning).\n- **Illusions** demonstrate perception ≠ registration: **Müller-Lyer, Ehrenstein, Ebbinghaus, Kanizsa triangle** (illusory contours from incomplete figures).\n- **Bistable / figure-ground figures** (duck-rabbit, young-old, Rubin): the first perceptual decision is **what is figure vs ground**, shaped by attention (Kanizsa 1975).\n- **Attention** (William James) = selecting some stimuli while ignoring others.\n- 🔥 **Inattentional blindness** (Simons & Chabris, 1999) = failure to consciously perceive an unattended (visible) stimulus — the \"invisible gorilla.\"\n\n### Types of attention 🧠 \"Selective · Sustained · Divided\"\n| Type | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| **Selective** | Pick one source/type of stimulus, exclude others |\n| **Sustained** | Maintain attention over long periods (vigilance) |\n| **Divided** | Distribute attention across several stimuli/tasks at once |\n- **Neural basis:** prefrontal + parietal cortex + subcortical (reticular system = alertness; posterior system = visual selective attention).\n\n---\n\n# 4️⃣ NEGLECT — Unilateral Spatial Neglect 🔥🔥\n> **A failure to attend to stimuli in the space *contralateral* to a brain lesion — most often the LEFT hemifield after a RIGHT (parietal) lesion — in the ABSENCE of any sensory or motor deficit.**\n\n- 🔥 **\"Right brain → Left neglect.\"** The **right hemisphere directs spatial attention to *both* sides**, while the left mainly attends to the right; so a **right-parietal lesion** leaves the left side unattended (severe, common). Left-hemisphere lesions rarely cause significant right neglect.\n- It's an **attention/representation deficit, NOT blindness or paralysis** — the visual pathways and muscles work; the patient just doesn't *attend* to that side.\n- 🔥 **Anosognosia** — the patient is typically **unaware of the deficit**; they **ignore objects, food, or people on the neglected side** (eats half a plate, shaves half the face, draws half a clock).\n- **Tests:** line bisection, cancellation tasks, clock drawing, copying a figure (left half omitted).\n\n### 🎯 MCQs\n1. **A right-hemisphere (parietal) lesion typically causes…** → *neglect of the LEFT side (contralateral).*\n2. **Neglect occurs in the absence of…** → *sensory or motor deficits* (it's an attentional disorder).\n3. **Patients with neglect are often…** → *unaware of it (anosognosia).*\n\n---\n\n# 5️⃣ Memory (neuro recap)\n- **Short-term memory:** capacity **7 ± 2 items** (Miller, 1956); **chunking** expands it; assessed by **span** + **recency/primacy** (serial position — Glanzer & Cunitz, 1966). Forgetting is mainly by **interference/attention shift**, not pure decay. *(89% of normals have a forward span of 5–8.)*\n- 🔑 **Working memory (Baddeley & Hitch, 1974):** **central executive** + **phonological loop** (verbal) + **visuospatial sketchpad** + **episodic buffer** (interface to LTM). *(Same model as your Memory lecture — 🧠 \"Captain Picard Visits Earth.\")*\n\n---\n\n# 6️⃣ Language & Aphasia 🔥\n> **Aphasia = acquired loss/alteration of language from brain damage** to the hemispheric language structures.\n- **Anterior system = Broca's area** (posterior inferior frontal gyrus) → **expression**.\n- **Posterior system = Wernicke's area** (superior/middle temporal + supramarginal/angular gyri) → **comprehension/retention**.\n\n| | **Non-fluent (Broca's)** | **Fluent (Wernicke's)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Output | **Reduced** (<30–40 wpm), effortful, **agrammatic/\"telegraphic\"** | **Normal/↑** (up to logorrhoea), well-articulated |\n| Prosody | Lost, laboured | Normal \"melody\" |\n| Meaning | Relatively preserved intent | **Impaired** — paraphasias, neologisms, jargon |\n| Comprehension | Relatively preserved | **Impaired** |\n| Awareness | Aware, frustrated | Often **unaware** (anosognosia), angry at not being understood |\n\n> 🧠 **\"Broca's = Broken speech (non-fluent); Wernicke's = Wordy but meaningless (fluent).\"**\n\n**Other syndromes:** **Global** (non-fluent + comprehension impaired, all perisylvian areas) · **Anomic** (\"afasia nominum\" — word-finding only, fluent, normal comprehension) · **Conduction** (fluent, normal comprehension, **impaired repetition** — arcuate fasciculus lesion between Broca & Wernicke).\n\n---\n\n# 7️⃣ Executive functions\nHigher-order processes for **planning, organising, controlling and regulating goal-directed behaviour** — problem solving, working memory, **cognitive flexibility**, **inhibition**, attentional control. Seat = **prefrontal cortex** (\"command centre\"). Assessed components: planning, monitoring, inhibitory control, set-shifting, executive supervision. *(See Stroop test, §11.)*\n\n---\n\n# 8️⃣ APRAXIA 🔥🔥\n> **Loss of the ability to perform purposeful, learned gestures/actions despite intact motor execution, coordination and sensation (no paralysis, ataxia, dystonia) and full awareness/comprehension.** It is a disorder of **motor programming** (planning, organisation, sequencing of movement).\n\n| Type | Core deficit | 🧠 Hook |\n|---|---|---|\n| 🔥 **Ideational** | Cannot **mentally construct the program/plan** of the action (esp. multi-step sequences/tool use) — the *concept* of what to do is lost | **\"No IDEA what to do\"** |\n| 🔥 **Ideomotor** | The **plan is intact** but the patient **cannot execute it on command** (a purposeful movement to verbal command) — though it may occur automatically | **\"Idea OK, but the Motor act fails on command\"** (voluntary–automatic dissociation) |\n| **Constructional** | Cannot **assemble parts into a meaningful whole** (drawing, copying, building) | parietal \"putting-together\" |\n| **Bucco-facial / oro-facial** | Cannot perform **face/mouth gestures** on command | (listed in the function map) |\n\n### 🔥 The exam contrast (ideomotor vs ideational)\n- **Ideational** = the **plan itself is gone** → can't conceptualise/sequence the action (what + the order).\n- **Ideomotor** = the **plan exists** but **can't be translated into the act on command** → the breakdown is at *execution of the voluntary motor program*, classically with preserved automatic/spontaneous performance.\n\n### 🎯 MCQs\n1. **Ideomotor apraxia =** *inability to carry out a purposeful movement on command despite an intact mental action plan.*\n2. **Ideational apraxia =** *inability to mentally construct/sequence the program of the action.*\n3. **Apraxia requires that…** *motor, sensory and coordination systems are intact and the patient is aware* (else it's paralysis/ataxia/dementia, not apraxia).\n\n---\n\n# 9️⃣ Agnosias\n> **Inability to recognise objects despite intact sensory input** — objects are perceived as \"presences\" but not recognised as \"concepts.\" By modality: **visual, auditory, tactile, somatoagnosia.** *(Praxis = doing; gnosis = recognising.)*\n\n---\n\n# 🔟 Neuropsychological assessment 🔥 📚 (what / why / when — incl. the 3 readings)\n**What it is:** administration & interpretation of **standardised tests** (with normative tables) probing orientation, language, memory, executive/attention, **praxis, gnosis**, perception, visuospatial function, insight — **plus qualitative observation of behaviour** (quantitative **and** qualitative data). It gives an **anatomo-clinical correlation** between deficits and brain damage.\n\n**Why/when:** diagnosis of lesions, **baseline + longitudinal monitoring**, differentiating dementia subtypes, treatment planning, rehabilitation, determining **functional ability**.\n\n### 📚 From the readings\n- **Casaletto & Heaton (2017), *Neuropsychological Assessment: Past & Future*:** traces the main assessment **approaches** — **Lurian** (qualitative, syndrome-based), **Fixed batteries** (e.g. Halstead-Reitan), **Flexible batteries**, and the **Boston Process Approach** (how the patient solves the task, not just the score). Key interpretive issues: **normative standards, cultural considerations, longitudinal change, common-metric batteries**, and the move toward **technology/digital** assessment and **cross-disciplinary integration**.\n- **Schaefer, Farrer & Dowling (2024), *Neuropsychology ↔ Primary Care collaboration*:** neuropsychologists are experts in **brain–behaviour relationships**; referrals are most efficient when the physician **states a clear referral question**, orders relevant labs/imaging, screens for treatable causes, and shares history. NP assessment aids **diagnosis, baseline, treatment planning, and functional-status determination** → better outcomes & satisfaction.\n- **Harsh et al. (2025), *Neuropsychology of Chronic Neurological Disorders*:** across **Alzheimer's, Parkinson's, epilepsy, MS, stroke, TBI**, cognitive deficits (memory, attention, executive, language) and **emotional disturbances** (depression, anxiety, **apathy**, mood dysregulation) are **core, interdependent features** — argues for **multidisciplinary, patient-centred** neuropsychological care.\n\n---\n\n# 1️⃣1️⃣ Dementias & differential diagnosis\n**Dementia** = diffuse decline of higher cortical functions (memory, language, reasoning, orientation) + personality/behavioural change, progressive (~10–12 yrs).\n- **MCI (Mild Cognitive Impairment)** = transitional stage between normal ageing and dementia; isolated subclinical deficit, **daily functioning preserved**, at risk of progression.\n- **Stages (e.g. Alzheimer's):** Mild (1–3 yr: recent-memory loss, word-finding, denial) → Moderate (3–6 yr) → Severe.\n\n| Disorder | Neuropsych hallmark |\n|---|---|\n| **Alzheimer's** | Early **anterograde memory** loss → aphasia/apraxia/agnosia |\n| **Parkinson's** | Motor (tremor, rigidity); **REM sleep behaviour disorder precedes PD in ~65%** |\n| **Lewy body** | **Fluctuating** attention/alertness, **visual hallucinations**, parkinsonism; memory relatively spared early; pentagon-copying on MMSE helps separate from AD |\n| **Huntington's chorea** | Involuntary movements + cognitive decline (autosomal dominant, 50% offspring risk) |\n| **Depressive pseudodementia** | Looks like dementia but: **depression first**, **no aphasia/apraxia**, **anhedonia**, cognition **responds to antidepressants** |\n\n### Classic test — the **Stroop effect** 🔥\nNaming the **ink colour** of a colour-word is **slower when incongruent** (word ≠ colour) → shows reading is **automatic** and irrelevant info is processed anyway → measures **inhibition / selective attention** (executive function).\n\n---\n---\n\n# 🎯 Rapid-fire MCQ bank (with answers)\n1. **Neuropsychology studies…** *the relationship between brain and behaviour/cognition.*\n2. **Clinical vs experimental neuropsychology?** *Clinical = brain-injured patients + diagnosis/rehab; experimental = healthy brains in the lab.*\n3. **Unilateral spatial neglect after a right-hemisphere lesion affects…** *the LEFT side*, with **anosognosia**, no sensory/motor loss.\n4. **Inattentional blindness (Simons & Chabris 1999) =** *failing to perceive an unattended visible stimulus.*\n5. **Ideational apraxia =** *can't construct the mental plan of the action.*\n6. **Ideomotor apraxia =** *can't execute the action on command though the plan is intact.*\n7. **Constructional apraxia =** *can't assemble parts into a meaningful whole.*\n8. **Broca's aphasia =** *non-fluent/expressive (agrammatic), comprehension relatively preserved.*\n9. **Wernicke's aphasia =** *fluent but meaningless (paraphasias/neologisms), comprehension impaired.*\n10. **Conduction aphasia =** *fluent, good comprehension, impaired repetition (arcuate fasciculus).*\n11. **Agnosia =** *failure to recognise objects despite intact sensation.*\n12. **Working memory components (Baddeley) =** *central executive + phonological loop + visuospatial sketchpad + episodic buffer.*\n13. **Pseudodementia is distinguished from dementia by…** *depression first, no aphasia/apraxia, anhedonia, response to antidepressants.*\n14. **Stroop effect measures…** *inhibition/selective attention (reading is automatic).*\n\n---\n\n# 🧩 Coverage map — exam recollection → this guide\n| Recalled exam item | Section |\n|---|---|\n| 🔥 \"Lesion of right side → neglecting left side\" | §4 |\n| 🔥 \"Ideomotor and ideational\" apraxia | §8 |\n| Inattentional blindness | §3 (also General Psychology) |\n| \"Triple network model / elements / CEN function\" | → in your **PNEI** section (DMN/SN/CEN) |\n\n---\n\n# 📚 Sourced readings (full citations)\n- **Casaletto, K. B. & Heaton, R. K. (2017).** *Neuropsychological Assessment: Past and Future.* *J Int Neuropsychol Soc*, 23(9–10), 778–790. → §10 (assessment approaches & history).\n- **Schaefer, L. A., Farrer, T. J. & Dowling, D. J. (2024).** *Improving the Effectiveness of Collaboration Between Neuropsychology and Primary Care.* *Prim Care Companion CNS Disord*, 26(5), 24nr03766. → §10 (referral/collaboration).\n- **Harsh, S. et al. (2025).** *The Neuropsychology of Chronic Neurological Disorders: A Review of Cognitive and Emotional Impairments.* *Cureus*, 17(10), e93642. → §10–11 (CND profiles, emotional impairments).\n- Lecture: *Clinical Neuropsychology & Neuropsychological Assessment in Neurodegenerative Diseases* (S. Oliveri, DISS, UniMi, a.a. 2025/26).\n\n---\n*That's all four branches done (General · Clinical · Social · Neuropsychology). Next per your message: I'll add the **book/paper source-mapping** to the General Psychology and Clinical guides so every topic is tied to its primary source.*\n"},"nephrology":{"title":"Nephrology — FCM1 Oral Exam Guide","text":"**NEPHROLOGY**\n\n**FCM1 ORAL EXAM MASTER GUIDE**\n\nFull 0-to-1 Depth · Built from your lecture notes\n\nAll 44 examined questions answered at full depth — every answer can survive follow-ups.\n\n**Every answer is built: WHAT IT IS → WHY IT HAPPENS → WHAT YOU SEE → DIAGNOSIS → TREATMENT → MEMORY TRICK.**\n\n*The old 'briefly brushed' topics (RTA, Alport, Goodpasture, contrast nephropathy, rhabdomyolysis, tumour lysis, MCD, FSGS, MPGN, lupus nephritis, ANCA vasculitides) are now FULL sections (Part 9). Only genuinely un-examined material is the short appendix (Part 11).*\n\n*All text is dark ink on light fills — nothing turns invisible.*\n\n# **CONTENTS**\n\n**Part 1:  **Chronic Kidney Disease (Q1–Q10)\n\n**Part 2:  **Glomerular Function & Urinalysis (Q11–Q15)\n\n**Part 3:  **Nephrotic Syndrome (Q16–Q18)\n\n**Part 4:  **Nephritic Syndrome & Glomerulonephritis (Q19–Q25)\n\n**Part 5:  **Acute Kidney Injury & Electrolytes (Q26–Q33)\n\n**Part 6:  **Blood Pressure, UTI & Stones (Q34–Q37)\n\n**Part 7:  **Diabetic Nephropathy, RAS, PKD (Q38–Q40)\n\n**Part 8:  **Clinical Cases (Q41–Q44)\n\n**Part 9:  **High-Yield Topics at Full Depth (formerly brushed over)\n\n**Part 10:  **Electrolytes & Acid-Base Deep Reference + Dialysis/Transplant\n\n**Part 11:  **Know-It-Exists Appendix (rarely examined)\n\n**★:  **Quick Reference — Numbers, Patterns & Memory Hooks\n\n# **PART 1 · CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE (CKD)**\n\n**Q1:  ****CKD exact definition · who is most at risk · why these patients become hypertensive**\n\n**DEFINITION (memorise word-for-word)  **CKD = either of the following present for ≥3 MONTHS:\n\n- **GFR ****<**** 60 mL/min/1.73m² (normal GFR ≈ 120; filtered volume ≈ 180 L/day), OR**\n\n- **Kidney damage ≥3 months — shown by pathological abnormalities OR markers of damage (abnormal blood/urine, e.g. albuminuria, or abnormal imaging) — even if GFR is still normal.**\n\n**⭐  The '3 months' is what makes it CHRONIC. Same numbers for ****<****3 months would be acute. Always say the duration.**\n\n### **Who is most at risk — the 3 big causes**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — 'Diabetes Has Got the kidneys' (DM ****>**** HTN ****>**** GN)**\n\nDiabetes mellitus — 25% of CKD, and 50% of ESRD (the #1 cause)\n\nHypertension — 2nd most common, 37% of ESRD cases\n\nGlomerulonephritis — 13% of ESRD cases\n\nOther susceptibility factors: older age, family history, low birth weight (fewer nephrons), certain ethnicities, low socioeconomic status.\n\n### **WHY CKD patients become hypertensive — 3 mechanisms**\n\n**🔑  Build the chain, don't memorise a list**\n\n1) RAAS activation: ↓GFR → slower tubular flow → more Na⁺ reabsorbed in the PCT → LOW salt delivery to the macula densa → the kidney is fooled into thinking perfusion is low → renin release → angiotensin II (vasoconstriction) + aldosterone (Na/water retention) → ↑BP.\n\n2) Sodium & water retention: damaged kidneys simply can't excrete Na⁺ → volume expansion → ↑BP.\n\n3) Loss of vasodilators: damaged kidneys make less prostaglandins and nitric oxide → unopposed vasoconstriction → ↑BP.\n\n**⭐  Hypertension is both a CAUSE and a CONSEQUENCE of CKD — a vicious cycle. Say that line; examiners love it.**\n\n**Q2:  ****Monitoring parameters (GFR + albuminuria) · why they differ · 5 stages · eGFR · failure threshold**\n\n**Two parameters, two different jobs  **\n\n- GFR = a measure of FUNCTION (how much the kidney is filtering right now).\n\n- Albuminuria = a measure of DAMAGE/injury, and it is the better PROGNOSTIC marker (predicts how fast things will get worse).\n\n### **Why GFR-alone differs from GFR + albuminuria**\n\n- ↓GFR ALONE (no albuminuria) → suggests the damage is more tubular/haemodynamic → slower progression, lower cardiovascular risk.\n\n- ↓GFR WITH albuminuria → means the glomerulus is leaking → faster progression to ESRD and much higher cardiovascular risk.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Albuminuria is the 'speedometer'**\n\nGFR tells you WHERE you are on the road to ESRD; albuminuria tells you HOW FAST you're driving there. Always report both.\n\n### **The 5 stages of CKD (by GFR)**\n\n| **Stage** | **GFR (mL/min/1.73m²)** | **Name** | **Key action** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **1** | ≥ 90 | Normal/high + damage present | Treat cause, slow progression |\n| **2** | 60–89 | Mild ↓ | Estimate progression rate |\n| **3a** | 45–59 | Mild–moderate ↓ | Evaluate complications |\n| **3b** | 30–44 | Moderate–severe ↓ | START: EPO, treat anaemia + hyperparathyroidism |\n| **4** | 15–29 | Severe ↓ | Prepare for dialysis / transplant |\n| **5** | < 15 (or on dialysis) | Kidney failure / ESRD | Dialysis or transplant |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — The magic number is 45**\n\n45 mL/min (the 3a→3b line) = where the kidney's ENDOCRINE function starts failing → this is when anaemia and bone disease begin, so EPO and bone management START at 3b.\n\n15 mL/min = the threshold for kidney FAILURE (Stage 5 / ESRD).\n\n**eGFR estimation  **Estimated from serum creatinine + age + sex (± race) using CKD-EPI, Cockcroft-Gault or MDRD formulas. Creatinine alone is imperfect — it depends on muscle mass, age, sex.\n\n**Q3:  ****General assessment of CKD: epidemiology, classification, risk factors**\n\n**Epidemiology  **Rising fast — now ~18th leading cause of death globally (was 27th in 1990). ~2 million on dialysis worldwide. ~11% of people are unaware they already have proteinuria/hypertension.\n\n### **Classification BY CAUSE**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — 'DV-GTIP' — the causes from glomerulus outward**\n\nD = Diabetic nephropathy (25% CKD / 50% ESRD)\n\nV = Vascular / hypertension (37% ESRD)\n\nG = Glomerulonephritis (13% ESRD)\n\nT = Tubular (acute tubular necrosis, tubulopathies)\n\nI = Interstitial (pyelonephritis, interstitial nephritis)\n\nP = Polycystic kidney disease\n\n### **Risk factors — 4 categories (the timeline of disease)**\n\n| **Category** | **Factors** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Susceptibility** | Older age, family history, low birth weight, ethnicity, low socioeconomic status |\n| **Initiation** | Diabetes, hypertension, autoimmune disease, infections, stones, obstruction, nephrotoxic drugs |\n| **Progression** | Proteinuria (the KEY modifiable one), uncontrolled BP/glucose, smoking |\n| **End-stage** | Low dialysis dose, anaemia, low albumin, late referral to nephrology |\n\n**Q4:  ****Consequences (complications) of CKD**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — 'HAM-U BEEP' — the CKD complications**\n\nH = Hypertension (secondary)\n\nA = Acidosis (metabolic)\n\nM = Mineral bone disease (secondary hyperparathyroidism)\n\nU = Uraemia\n\nB = Bleeding (platelet dysfunction)\n\nE = Encephalopathy (uraemic)\n\nE = Erythropoietin deficiency → anaemia\n\nP = Pericarditis\n\n### **1) Metabolic acidosis (pH can fall ****<****7.2)**\n\n- Mechanism: ↓GFR → kidney can't excrete H⁺ or reabsorb enough HCO₃⁻ → H⁺ rises, HCO₃⁻ falls → acidosis.\n\n- Knock-on effects: H⁺/K⁺ exchange pushes K⁺ out of cells → HYPERKALAEMIA; chemoreceptors sense low pH → tachypnoea (Kussmaul breathing).\n\n- Treatment: oral sodium bicarbonate.\n\n### **2) Hyperkalaemia (K⁺ ****>**** 5.3 mEq/L)**\n\n- Mechanism: ↓GFR → less K⁺ filtered + less distal secretion.\n\n- **ECG progression (learn the order!): peaked T waves → long PR (AV-block risk) → loss of P waves + wide QRS → sine wave → V-fib → arrest.**\n\n- Treatment ladder: low-K⁺ diet → loop diuretic if hypervolaemic → K⁺-binding resin (drives K⁺ into stool).\n\n### **3) Hypervolaemia**\n\n- ↓GFR → Na⁺/water retention → pulmonary oedema + pleural effusion + peripheral oedema + cardiac strain. Monitor by DAILY WEIGHT. Symptoms: dyspnoea, pitting oedema, raised JVP.\n\n### **4) Uraemia (when azotaemia becomes symptomatic)**\n\n- Brain → uraemic encephalopathy: altered mental status + ASTERIXIS (flapping tremor on wrist extension).\n\n- Heart → uraemic PERICARDITIS: friction rub + chest pain ± effusion; ECG = diffuse ST elevation + PR depression.\n\n- Platelets → dysfunction → bleeding tendency.\n\nPlus: secondary hypertension, renal anaemia, secondary hyperparathyroidism (each detailed below).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — The 'Rule of 7' = a CKD emergency**\n\nConcurrent: pH < 7  +  K⁺ > 7 mEq/L  +  PO₄ > 7 mg/dL.\n\nAll three '7's together = emergency hospitalisation (urgent dialysis).\n\n**Q5:  ****Hormonal (endocrine) functions of the kidney**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — The kidney makes 'REV' hormones**\n\nR = Renin — from the juxtaglomerular apparatus → starts the RAAS → controls Na balance + BP.\n\nE = Erythropoietin (EPO) — stimulates RBC maturation in bone marrow (this is why CKD causes anaemia).\n\nV = Vitamin D activation — 1-alpha-hydroxylase makes 1,25-(OH)₂-D₃ (calcitriol) → controls calcium/phosphate (this is why CKD causes bone disease).\n\n**⭐  These three explain THREE complications of CKD: ↓renin control → hypertension dysregulation; ↓EPO → anaemia; ↓vitamin D → secondary hyperparathyroidism. Endocrine function fails first, around GFR 45 (Stage 3b).**\n\n**Q6:  ****Hypertension in CKD patients**\n\nSee Q1 for the 3 mechanisms (RAAS, Na/water retention, lost vasodilators). The key clinical points to add:\n\n- **BP control is the MOST IMPORTANT treatment target in CKD — it protects both heart and kidney.**\n\n- First-line drugs = ACE inhibitors / ARBs. They lower BP AND reduce intraglomerular pressure (by dilating the efferent arteriole) → slow progression + reduce proteinuria.\n\n- Expect a small rise in creatinine (up to 30%) and K⁺ when starting an ACEi — this is the drug PROTECTING the glomerulus. It plateaus over 2–3 months. STOP only if creatinine rises >30%.\n\n- ACEi contraindications: bilateral renal artery stenosis, volume depletion, concurrent NSAIDs.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — BP goals shift with proteinuria**\n\nMinimal proteinuria → target < 130/80.\n\nProteinuria up to 1 g/day → tighter target < 125/75.\n\n**Q7:  ****Anaemia in CKD: why not let Hb get too high · thrombosis · definition · HCT risk threshold**\n\n**Definition of anaemia  **Men: Hb < 13 g/dL.  Women: Hb < 12 g/dL.  Renal anaemia is normochromic, normocytic.\n\n- **It appears at Stage 3b (GFR ****<**** 45) — when endocrine function fails. If anaemia appears BEFORE 3b (GFR ****>**** 45), it is NOT renal — look elsewhere (iron deficiency, GI bleed, marrow disease).**\n\n### **Why we deliberately DON'T fully normalise Hb**\n\n- **Target is a PERSONALISED 11–12.5 g/dL using iron + recombinant EPO — NOT normal Hb.**\n\n- Over-treating with EPO to reach normal Hb → blood gets too viscous (HCT can reach 80–90%) → THROMBOSIS → stroke or MI.\n\n- EPO also stimulates angiogenesis → can fuel growth/recurrence of existing tumours.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — HCT danger zone**\n\nHaematocrit climbing toward 55%+ (and especially 80–90% with EPO overshoot) = high thrombosis risk. Aim for 'enough, not normal.'\n\n**Acute vs chronic feel  **Chronic CKD patients adapt to slow-onset anaemia and feel little. AKI patients drop Hb fast (14 → 8 g/dL in 24–48h) and feel severe symptoms.\n\n**Why treat early (start at 3b)  **Untreated anaemia → tissue hypoxia → peripheral vasodilation → ↓BP → RAAS activation → fluid retention → ↑cardiac afterload → LEFT VENTRICULAR HYPERTROPHY. Most CKD deaths are cardiac, so EPO+iron from Stage 3b prevents LVH and CV death.\n\n**Q8:  ****Renal anaemia: how HIGH PTH causes anaemia**\n\nRenal anaemia has several causes (↓EPO, iron deficiency via hepcidin, chronic inflammation, short RBC lifespan in uraemia). The specific PTH link:\n\n**🔑  Two ways high PTH worsens anaemia**\n\n1) Bone-marrow crowding: very high PTH drives massive bone turnover → marrow fibrosis crowds out RBC production.\n\n2) Mechanical haemolysis: high PTH → CKD-MBD → vascular calcification → stiff, narrow vessels → RBCs sheared as they squeeze through → premature haemolysis.\n\n**Iron / hepcidin angle (worth a sentence)  **CKD = chronic inflammation → IL-6 → liver makes hepcidin → hepcidin blocks iron release from macrophages and iron absorption from gut → functional iron deficiency. That's why we check ferritin >100 µg/L and transferrin sat >20% before blaming EPO deficiency.\n\n**Q9:  ****Phosphate–calcium–vitamin D · secondary hyperparathyroidism · CKD-MBD · FGF23–Klotho axis**\n\n**Normal PTH  **15–65 pg/mL at normal calcium. PTH is released by parathyroid chief cells when: calcium is LOW, phosphate is HIGH, or calcitriol is LOW.\n\n**What PTH does  **Bone → resorbs bone to release Ca²⁺ + PO₄. Kidney → reabsorbs Ca²⁺, dumps PO₄ (phosphaturia), and makes more calcitriol (via 1-alpha-hydroxylase). Calcitriol → absorbs Ca²⁺ + PO₄ from gut.\n\n### **How CKD causes secondary hyperparathyroidism — the chain**\n\n**🔑  Follow the dominoes**\n\n1) Smaller kidney → can't excrete phosphate → HYPERPHOSPHATAEMIA, and can't make calcitriol (↓1-alpha-hydroxylase) → low vitamin D.\n\n2) Hypocalcaemia develops two ways: (a) high PO₄ binds free Ca²⁺ into insoluble complexes; (b) low calcitriol → less gut Ca²⁺ absorption. (Note: kidney failure does NOT lower calcium directly.)\n\n3) Low Ca²⁺ + high PO₄ + low calcitriol → parathyroids pump out PTH and enlarge (hyperplasia) = secondary hyperparathyroidism (PTH can hit 200–1000 pg/mL).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — The PARADOX**\n\nThe body's fix for low calcium (PTH-driven bone resorption) releases BOTH calcium AND phosphate — so it worsens the hyperphosphataemia that started the problem. A self-feeding cycle.\n\n### **The FGF23–Klotho axis (the high-yield modern bit)**\n\n- High phosphate load → bone makes FGF23. FGF23 is phosphaturic (dumps PO₄) and inhibits 1-alpha-hydroxylase (lowers calcitriol). It basically fights PTH from the phosphate side.\n\n- FGF23 needs KLOTHO (a co-receptor made in kidney) to work. As CKD worsens, Klotho falls → FGF23 becomes less effective → phosphate stays high → even MORE FGF23 and PTH.\n\n- **Extra danger: FGF23 binds FGFR4 on heart cells → directly causes LEFT VENTRICULAR HYPERTROPHY.**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — FGF23 vs PTH = a tug-of-war over phosphate**\n\nPTH and FGF23 both try to dump phosphate, but FGF23 ALSO kills vitamin D (lowering calcium) and needs Klotho — which CKD destroys. So the more advanced the CKD, the more the system breaks and phosphate wins.\n\n### **Telling the hyperparathyroidisms apart (a classic follow-up)**\n\n| **Type** | **Calcium** | **Phosphate** | **PTH** | **Story** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Primary** | HIGH | LOW | mildly ↑ (≤100) | Gland problem (adenoma) — autonomous from the start |\n| **Secondary** | LOW | HIGH | hugely ↑ (to 1000) | Reaction to CKD — gland is responding correctly |\n| **Tertiary** | HIGH | HIGH | ↑ & resistant | Long-standing secondary → gland becomes autonomous |\n\n**Treatment of secondary HPT  **Limit dietary phosphate (<800 mg/day) + phosphate binders + vitamin D to suppress PTH. Do NOT give calcium — the problem is absorption, not scarcity.\n\n**Bone/vessel damage to name  **Renal osteodystrophy (osteitis fibrosa cystica, 'salt-and-pepper' skull, zebra/brown-tumour bones); calciphylaxis (calcified vessels → skin ulcers, also seen with warfarin); vascular calcification = Mönckeberg (tunica media) vs atherosclerosis (tunica intima).\n\n**Q10:  ****End-stage kidney disease (ESRD): what metabolic changes happen and why**\n\n**Definition  **ESRD = needs dialysis or transplant. GFR < 15. Characterised by oliguria, uraemia (often anaemia), salt/water retention with hypervolaemia ± nephritic oedema + hypertension.\n\nEach change traces back to 'the kidney can no longer filter or perform its endocrine jobs':\n\n| **Change** | **Why it happens** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Metabolic acidosis** | Can't excrete H⁺ or reabsorb HCO₃⁻ → acid builds up |\n| **Hyperkalaemia** | Can't filter/secrete K⁺ → K⁺ rises (cardiac arrhythmia risk) |\n| **Hyperphosphataemia + hypocalcaemia** | Can't excrete PO₄ + can't activate vitamin D → bone disease |\n| **Uraemia / ↑BUN** | Can't excrete nitrogenous waste → encephalopathy, pericarditis, bleeding |\n| **Anaemia** | No EPO production |\n| **Hypervolaemia + hypertension** | Can't excrete Na⁺/water → volume overload + RAAS dysregulation |\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Indications that mean 'start ESRD protocol now'**\n\nRefractory acidosis · refractory hyperkalaemia · refractory hypervolaemia · uraemic complications · Stage 5 GFR <15.\n\nESRD treatment = substitutive (dialysis or transplant) OR conservative (symptom management + end-of-life care). Detailed dialysis/transplant content is in Q40 and the dialysis topic.\n\n# **PART 2 · GLOMERULAR FUNCTION ****&**** URINALYSIS**\n\n**Q11:  ****How to check glomerular function (GFR, haematuria, proteinuria, urinary sediments)**\n\nFour complementary tools — each looks at a different thing:\n\n| **Tool** | **What it tells you** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **GFR** | Overall filtering capacity (function). Best single number for 'how much kidney is left.' |\n| **Proteinuria / albuminuria** | Integrity of the filtration barrier (damage + prognosis). Albumin in urine = earliest sign of barrier damage, often BEFORE GFR falls. |\n| **Haematuria** | Whether blood is leaking — and the RBC SHAPE tells you if the source is glomerular or urological. |\n| **Urinary sediment** | Microscope look at cells, casts, crystals — can diagnose without a biopsy, and it's cheap + informative. |\n\n**⭐  Order of thinking: GFR = how much function; albuminuria = how damaged + how fast it'll worsen; sediment = WHAT kind of damage. Together they often replace a biopsy.**\n\n**Q12:  ****How to measure GFR · when precise single-kidney GFR matters**\n\n### **Ways to measure/estimate GFR (least → most precise)**\n\n| **Method** | **Notes** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Serum creatinine (eGFR)** | Cheapest, routine. Logarithmic relationship to GFR and OVER-estimates by 10–20%. Affected by muscle mass, age, sex, race. |\n| **Urine creatinine / creatinine clearance** | 24-hour urine collection; better than serum alone. |\n| **Inulin clearance** | GOLD STANDARD — freely filtered, not secreted/reabsorbed. Research/precise settings only. |\n| **Nuclear medicine (renal scintigraphy)** | Measures SPLIT function — how much each kidney contributes separately. |\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — When is precise SINGLE-kidney GFR critical?**\n\nBefore donating/removing a kidney (living donor work-up) and before nephrectomy for tumour — you must know how much each kidney works separately, so nuclear-medicine split GFR is essential.\n\nFor living donors: both kidneys are assessed separately and the WORSE one is given, to minimise harm to the donor.\n\n**Adjust GFR/creatinine to the patient  **Muscular → higher normal creatinine. Elderly → GFR falls ~1 mL/min/1.73m²/year after 60. Pregnant → lower normal creatinine (volume expansion).\n\n**Q13:  ****Urinary sediments: what they are · link to nephritic syndrome**\n\nSpin urine in a centrifuge, look under the microscope. What you can find:\n\n### **Cells**\n\n- RBCs (haematuria) → if DYSMORPHIC / acanthocytes under polarised light = GLOMERULAR source. If normal-shaped = urological (lower tract).\n\n- WBCs (leukocyturia) → infection/inflammation (cystitis, pyelonephritis). White urine = pyuria.\n\n- Epithelial cells → squamous (urethra/vagina, often normal); transitional (anywhere in tract); renal tubular (= tubular necrosis, transplant rejection, pyelonephritis).\n\n### **Casts — the high-yield part**\n\nCasts are moulds of the tubule made of Tamm-Horsfall protein. The cells trapped inside tell the story:\n\n| **Cast type** | **Means** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Hyaline** | Normal/benign (after exercise or stress) — transparent |\n| **RBC (erythrocyte) cast** | NEPHRITIC syndrome / glomerulonephritis — almost diagnostic |\n| **WBC (leukocyte) cast** | Pyelonephritis OR severe-inflammation glomerulonephritis (e.g. lupus) |\n| **Granular ('muddy brown')** | Acute tubular necrosis (ATN) |\n| **Fatty cast** | Nephrotic syndrome (lipiduria) |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Casts = WHO is stuck in the tube tells you WHERE the problem is**\n\nRBC cast = blood leaking through an inflamed glomerulus = NEPHRITIC.\n\nMuddy-brown granular cast = dead tubular cells = ATN.\n\nFatty cast = leaking lipids = NEPHROTIC.\n\nWBC cast = pus in the tube = pyelonephritis.\n\n**⭐  Link to nephritic syndrome: a single RBC cast (or 1–5 dysmorphic RBCs / acanthocytes per field) is enough to call the bleeding GLOMERULAR → nephritic. That's the interconnection examiners want.**\n\n### **Crystals (bonus)**\n\n- Low-pH urine: uric acid (hexagonal → gout), calcium oxalate (Maltese-cross → high oxalate diet).\n\n- High-pH urine: calcium phosphate (wedge), struvite (coffin-lid → urease-positive bacteria), cystine.\n\n**Q14:  ****Haematuria: glomerular vs urological · RBC casts/acanthocytes · major condition · RBC count for nephritic**\n\n**Definition  **> 3 RBCs per high-power field. Gross (visible, cola-coloured) vs microscopic (dipstick/microscope only).\n\n### **Glomerular vs urological — how to tell**\n\n| **Feature** | **GLOMERULAR (kidney)** | **UROLOGICAL (lower tract)** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **RBC shape** | Dysmorphic / acanthocytes | Normal, round |\n| **Casts** | RBC casts present | No casts |\n| **Urine colour** | Coca-Cola / brown | Bright red |\n| **Proteinuria** | Often present | Usually absent (or trivial) |\n| **Causes** | Glomerulonephritis, nephritic syndrome | Stones, infection, tumour, trauma, contamination by menses |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Cola = kidney, Red = road (lower tract)**\n\nCoca-Cola coloured + dysmorphic RBCs + casts = GLOMERULAR.\n\nBright red + normal RBCs + no casts = UROLOGICAL (think stone/infection/cancer).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Two specific exam answers**\n\n'Major condition showing haematuria' the examiner wants = a urinary tract CANCER (bladder/kidney) — always rule out malignancy in painless haematuria, especially older smokers.\n\n'How many RBCs to diagnose nephritic?' = even 1 RBC cast, or 1–5 dysmorphic RBCs / acanthocytes per high-power field, is enough.\n\n**💡  Vitamin C can cause a FALSE-NEGATIVE dipstick (it's a reducing agent that blocks the peroxidase colour reaction). Pseudohaematuria = dipstick positive but no RBCs on microscopy (think myoglobin in rhabdomyolysis, or haemoglobin in haemolysis).**\n\n**Q15:  ****Types of proteinuria · normal values · light chains in myeloma · biopsy threshold**\n\n**Normal  **< 1 g/day (some sources <150 mg). Albumin-to-creatinine ratio (ACR) normally < 30. Proteinuria is usually the FIRST sign of CKD and the best prognostic marker — control it and you slow CKD.\n\n### **The types (by where the protein comes from)**\n\n| **Type** | **Mechanism / proteins** | **Causes** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Glomerular** | Barrier leaks (heavier proteinuria, mostly albumin) | GN, diabetic nephropathy, hypertension, CKD |\n| **Tubular** | Tubule can't reabsorb small proteins (milder; α1/β2-microglobulin) | ATN, acute interstitial nephritis |\n| **Overflow / pre-renal** | Too much small protein floods the filter | Myeloma (Bence-Jones light chains), rhabdomyolysis (myoglobin), haemolysis (Hb) |\n| **Post-renal** | Protein added below the kidney | UTI, urinary tract tumours |\n\n**🔑  Why myeloma raises light chains (Bence-Jones)**\n\nMultiple myeloma = malignant clone of plasma cells → they overproduce a single (monoclonal) antibody and huge amounts of free light chains (kappa or lambda).\n\nThese small light chains are freely filtered and flood the urine = Bence-Jones proteinuria (an OVERFLOW proteinuria). They are also directly toxic to tubules (cast nephropathy → AKI).\n\nDetect with urine electrophoresis (UPEP) / serum free light chains — a standard dipstick can MISS light chains (it mainly detects albumin).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Proteinuria selectivity (electrophoresis)**\n\nSelective (MW <100,000 — mostly albumin + transferrin) → milder glomerular lesions.\n\nNon-selective (MW >100,000 — includes IgG) → more severe glomerular damage.\n\n### **When is proteinuria an indication for BIOPSY?**\n\n- **Any proteinuria ****>**** 1 g/day generally warrants biopsy — EXCEPT when it can be explained less invasively:**\n\n- Diabetic with retinopathy → assume diabetic nephropathy (biopsy only if NO retinopathy).\n\n- Child with nephrotic syndrome → almost always minimal change disease (trial steroids first).\n\n- Chronic nephropathy with small kidneys → US + creatinine enough.\n\n- Post-strep GN in a child (sore throat → haematuria 2 weeks later) → clinical diagnosis.\n\n- Biopsy CONTRAINDICATED: coagulation disorders, small kidneys, renal masses, obstructive nephropathy with hydronephrosis.\n\n- Always biopsy: AKI of unknown origin, adult nephrotic syndrome (>3.5 g/day), nephritic with persistent haematuria, congenital abnormalities.\n\n# **PART 3 · NEPHROTIC SYNDROME**\n\n**Q16:  ****Nephrotic syndrome: definition, characteristics, protein/albumin levels, causes, by age**\n\n**One-line idea  **Nephrotic = the glomerulus loses its protein wall → you PEE OUT PROTEIN. (Nephritic = you pee out BLOOD.)\n\n### **The 4 defining features**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — 'PALE' — the 4 features of nephrotic syndrome**\n\nP = Proteinuria, MASSIVE: > 3.5 g/day (this is the threshold number).\n\nA = Albumin low (hypoalbuminaemia): serum albumin < 3 g/dL → oedema starts. Below ~2.5 g/dL it's severe.\n\nL = Lipids high (hyperlipidaemia + lipiduria/fatty casts): the liver overproduces lipoproteins to replace lost protein.\n\nE = Edema: pitting, dependent, transudate — and classically PERIORBITAL (puffy eyes), which distinguishes it from heart/liver oedema.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — The numbers examiners check**\n\nProteinuria > 3.5 g/day = nephrotic threshold.\n\nAlbumin < 3 g/dL = oedema appears (the lower the albumin, the worse the oedema).\n\nBP is typically NORMAL in pure nephrotic (vs nephritic where it's HIGH).\n\n### **Why each feature happens (the chain)**\n\n- Barrier loses its negative charge + podocytes are damaged → albumin leaks out → MASSIVE proteinuria.\n\n- → serum albumin drops → oncotic pressure falls → fluid leaks into tissues → OEDEMA (periorbital, legs, ascites, pleural effusion).\n\n- → low albumin signals the LIVER to make more proteins, including lipoproteins → HYPERLIPIDAEMIA + fatty casts.\n\n- → the liver also overmakes clotting factors AND you lose anticoagulant proteins (antithrombin, protein C/S) in urine → HYPERCOAGULABLE.\n\n### **Causes — primary glomerular diseases BY AGE (high-yield)**\n\n| **Age group** | **Most likely primary nephrotic cause** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Children** | MINIMAL CHANGE DISEASE (≈90%) — responds to steroids |\n| **Adults** | MEMBRANOUS NEPHROPATHY (commonest primary adult nephrotic) |\n| **Adults (esp. African-American)** | FOCAL SEGMENTAL GLOMERULOSCLEROSIS (FSGS) |\n\n**Secondary (non-renal) causes  **Diabetes (commonest overall cause of nephrotic-range proteinuria), amyloidosis, lupus (membranous = lupus class V), infections (HBV/HCV, syphilis), drugs (gold, NSAIDs), malignancy.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Child vs Adult nephrotic**\n\nChild = Minimal Change ('Children Care less, Change Minimal') → steroids work.\n\nAdult = Membranous (the 'M' for Mature adults).\n\nDon't-forget overall = DIABETES is the most common cause of heavy proteinuria of all.\n\n**Q17:  ****Diagnosis of nephrotic syndrome**\n\n### **Step 1 — confirm it IS nephrotic (not nephritic)**\n\n- Protein:creatinine ratio > 3.5 OR 24-h urine protein > 3.5 g/day, PLUS lipiduria/fatty casts + hyperlipidaemia + hypoalbuminaemia + oedema → nephrotic.\n\n- (Contrast: < 3.5 g/day + haematuria with RBC casts + hypertension + ↓GFR = nephritic.)\n\n### **Step 2 — find the cause (non-invasive first)**\n\n| **Test → result** | **Diagnosis** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Chronic diabetes + HbA1c ****>****6.5% + retinopathy present** | Diabetic nephropathy (no biopsy needed) |\n| **Anti-PLA2R or anti-THSD7A positive** | Membranous nephropathy |\n| **SPEP/UPEP positive + fat-pad biopsy positive** | Amyloid nephropathy |\n\n### **Step 3 — biopsy (if resistant to treatment or no clear cause)**\n\n| **Biopsy finding** | **Diagnosis** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (light microscopy)** | Diabetic nephropathy |\n| **Apple-green birefringence on Congo red** | Renal amyloidosis |\n| **Thick GBM + subepithelial deposits + granular IF** | Membranous nephropathy |\n| **Sclerotic/hyalinotic glomeruli + podocyte effacement (EM)** | FSGS |\n| **Normal LM + podocyte foot-process fusion (EM only)** | Minimal change disease |\n\n**⭐  Remember: child + nephrotic → trial steroids WITHOUT biopsy first (almost always minimal change). Adult + nephrotic → biopsy. That's the practical rule.**\n\n**Q18:  ****Complications of nephrotic syndrome**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — 'PHIL gets CKD' — nephrotic complications**\n\nP = Protein loss effects (muscle wasting, malnutrition)\n\nH = Hyperlipidaemia → accelerated atherosclerosis (stroke, CAD)\n\nI = Infection (you pee out immunoglobulins → peritonitis, pneumonia, UTI)\n\nL = Lipiduria/edema (pleural effusion, periorbital, ascites, leg oedema)\n\n+ Hypercoagulability → DVT, PE, RENAL VEIN THROMBOSIS\n\n+ CKD (chronic injury → falling GFR, esp. FSGS & membranous)\n\n### **The two complications most worth explaining**\n\n**🔑  Why hypercoagulability + infection?**\n\nHypercoagulable: the leaky glomerulus loses natural anticoagulants (antithrombin III, protein C/S) in the urine, AND the liver over-makes clotting factors → clots. Highest risk in MEMBRANOUS nephropathy. Classic: renal vein thrombosis + DVT/PE.\n\nInfection-prone: you also lose immunoglobulins (antibodies) in the urine → low IgG → susceptible to encapsulated bacteria → spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, pneumonia, UTIs.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Don't confuse nephrotic oedema with heart/liver oedema**\n\nNephrotic oedema is classically PERIORBITAL (puffy around the eyes, worse in the morning) — heart failure oedema is dependent (ankles) and cirrhosis is ascites-predominant.\n\n# **PART 4 · NEPHRITIC SYNDROME ****&**** GLOMERULONEPHRITIS**\n\n**Q19:  ****Nephritic syndrome: presentation ****&**** causes · race differences in ACEi/ARB efficacy**\n\n**One-line idea  **Nephritic = the glomerulus is INFLAMED → it bleeds → you pee out BLOOD (with RBC casts) + a bit of protein + your BP goes up.\n\n### **Presentation — the features**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — 'HHOP-JI' nephritic features**\n\nH = Haematuria (cola-coloured, with RBC casts / dysmorphic RBCs)\n\nH = Hypertension\n\nO = Oliguria\n\nP = Proteinuria, MILD (< 2.5 g/day) → mild oedema\n\nJ = Jugular venous distension (fluid overload)\n\nI = ↑BUN (>18) and ↑Creatinine (>1.2) — i.e. falling GFR (azotaemia)\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Nephritic vs Nephrotic in one line**\n\nLosing BLOOD (haematuria + RBC casts + HTN + mild protein) = NEPHRITIC.\n\nLosing PROTEIN (>3.5 g/day + low albumin + lipids + normal BP) = NEPHROTIC.\n\n**Causes  **IgA nephropathy (commonest), post-streptococcal GN, rapidly progressive GN (anti-GBM, lupus, ANCA), membranoproliferative GN, Alport. (Detailed in Q22–25.)\n\n**🔑  Race difference in ACEi/ARB efficacy (the specific exam point)**\n\nACE inhibitors / ARBs work via the RAAS. Many African-American patients tend to have a LOWER-renin, salt-sensitive hypertension, so ACEi/ARB monotherapy is LESS effective at lowering BP in this group.\n\nPractical consequence: in Black patients, a calcium-channel blocker or thiazide is often more effective first-line for BP — BUT if there is proteinuric CKD/diabetes, an ACEi/ARB is still used for its kidney-protective (anti-proteinuric) effect, usually in combination.\n\n**Q20:  ****Difference between glomerulonephritis and acute nephritic syndrome**\n\n|  | **Glomerulonephritis (GN)** | **Acute nephritic SYNDROME** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **What it is** | The DISEASE/pathology — inflammation of the glomeruli (a tissue diagnosis) | The clinical PRESENTATION — the cluster of signs/symptoms (haematuria, HTN, oliguria, mild proteinuria, ↑Cr) |\n| **Level** | Histological / mechanistic | Clinical / syndromic |\n| **Relationship** | A specific GN (e.g. IgA, post-strep) is the CAUSE | ...that produces the nephritic SYNDROME |\n\n**⭐  Say it like this: 'Glomerulonephritis is the disease in the tissue; acute nephritic syndrome is how that disease shows up clinically. One is the cause, the other is the picture.'**\n\n**Also useful — inflammatory vs degenerative  **Glomerulopathy of INFLAMMATORY nature = glomeruloNEPHRITIS. Of DEGENERATIVE nature (no inflammation) = nephropathy (e.g. membranous nephropathy, diabetic nephropathy).\n\n**Q21:  ****Glomerulopathies: primary vs secondary · biopsy findings · focal/diffuse, segmental/global**\n\n### **Primary vs secondary**\n\n- PRIMARY = the kidney is the primary disease site (e.g. IgA nephropathy, membranous, FSGS, minimal change, MPGN, RPGN).\n\n- SECONDARY = kidney disease due to a systemic disease (diabetes, hypertension, lupus, amyloidosis, vasculitis, HBV/HCV, myeloma, Henoch-Schönlein purpura).\n\n### **The biopsy descriptive terms (examiners love these)**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Two questions: HOW MANY glomeruli? and HOW MUCH of each?**\n\nHOW MANY glomeruli are involved →  FOCAL = only SOME glomeruli  ·  DIFFUSE = ALL glomeruli.\n\nHOW MUCH of a single glomerulus →  SEGMENTAL = only PART of it  ·  GLOBAL = the WHOLE glomerulus.\n\n(Extra: MESANGIAL = only the mesangial cells involved.)\n\n| **Term** | **Meaning** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Focal** | < 50% of glomeruli affected |\n| **Diffuse** | > 50% / all glomeruli affected |\n| **Segmental** | Only part of an individual glomerulus |\n| **Global** | The entire glomerulus |\n\n**What the 3 biopsy 'lights' show  **Light microscopy (LM) = overall architecture (sclerosis, proliferation, crescents). Immunofluorescence (IF) = WHERE antibodies/complement deposit (linear vs granular vs absent). Electron microscopy (EM) = ultrastructure (deposits' location, podocyte effacement).\n\n**💡  IF pattern is the fastest classifier: LINEAR = anti-GBM disease; GRANULAR ('lumpy-bumpy') = immune-complex disease; ABSENT (pauci-immune) = ANCA vasculitis.**\n\n**Q22:  ****IgA nephropathy (Berger's disease)**\n\n**Why it matters  **The MOST COMMON glomerular disease worldwide. Classic nephritic picture.\n\n**🔑  Mechanism in plain steps**\n\nA mucosal infection (often a sore throat or GI bug) → the body makes abnormally glycosylated IgA1 (galactose-deficient) → the immune system sees it as foreign → makes IgG against it → IgG–IgA immune complexes form → they deposit in the glomerular MESANGIUM → activate complement (C3) → glomerular inflammation → haematuria.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — IgA = 'Infection-then-Aematuria, Almost immediately'**\n\nSYNPHARYNGITIC: haematuria appears DURING or 1–2 days AFTER the sore throat. (Contrast post-strep GN, which lags ~2 WEEKS.)\n\nThis timing is the single best discriminator from post-streptococcal GN.\n\n**Presentation  **Recurrent episodes of micro/macro-haematuria + fever + hypertension ± mild proteinuria ± oedema. Associated with Henoch-Schönlein purpura (same IgA, with skin/joint/GI involvement).\n\n**Diagnosis  **Serology: ↑serum IgA + normal/↑ C3 (C3 NOT low — very characteristic). IF: mesangial IgA deposition. LM: focal proliferative GN.\n\n**Treatment / course  **ACEi/ARB for BP + proteinuria, steroids if progressive. Can progress to CKD even when mild.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — C3 level splits the nephritic causes**\n\nNORMAL C3 + recent URTI/GI infection → IgA nephropathy.\n\nNORMAL C3 + deafness + eye lenticonus → Alport.\n\nLOW C3 + sore throat 2 WEEKS ago → post-streptococcal GN.\n\nLOW C3 + ANA/anti-dsDNA → lupus nephritis.\n\n**Q23:  ****Membranous nephropathy: antibody · main adult type · screening**\n\n**Why it matters  **The most common PRIMARY nephrotic syndrome in ADULTS.\n\n**🔑  Mechanism**\n\nAutoantibodies (IgG4) attack the podocyte → in PRIMARY disease the target antigen is PLA2R (phospholipase A2 receptor) — occasionally THSD7A.\n\nImmune complexes deposit in the SUBEPITHELIAL space → complement (MAC, C5b-9) damages podocytes → loss of slit diaphragm → massive proteinuria. GBM thickens. NO significant inflammation (it's a 'quiet' nephrotic picture).\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Membranous = 'Mature adults, PLA2R, Membrane thickens'**\n\nAntibody = anti-PLA2R (IgG4). Main adult primary nephrotic. Subepithelial 'spike-and-dome' deposits, thick GBM.\n\n**Screening / diagnosis  **Check anti-PLA2R (and anti-THSD7A) antibodies — a positive titre can diagnose primary membranous WITHOUT biopsy and is used to monitor treatment. IF: granular subepithelial IgG + C3. Serology: low C3.\n\n**Secondary causes to exclude  **Infections (HBV/HCV, syphilis), lupus (class V), MALIGNANCY (colorectal, lung, bladder — so screen for cancer!), drugs (gold, penicillamine).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Membranous = highest clot risk**\n\nMembranous nephropathy carries the HIGHEST risk of renal vein thrombosis / DVT / PE of all the nephrotic diseases. If a nephrotic patient suddenly gets flank pain + worsening function, think renal vein thrombosis.\n\n**Q24:  ****Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN)**\n\n**Definition  **A nephritic syndrome that destroys kidney function over DAYS to WEEKS, with CRESCENTS in Bowman's space on biopsy. A nephrological emergency.\n\n**🔑  Why crescents form**\n\nSevere glomerular capillary wall injury → plasma proteins + fibrin leak into Bowman's space → parietal epithelial cells proliferate + macrophages pile in → they form a CRESCENT shape that crushes the glomerulus → GFR collapses fast.\n\n### **The 3 types (classified by immunofluorescence — this is the key framework)**\n\n| **Type** | **IF pattern** | **Cause(s)** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Type 1** | LINEAR | Anti-GBM disease (Ab vs type-IV collagen). With lung haemoptysis = Goodpasture. |\n| **Type 2** | GRANULAR (immune complex) | Lupus nephritis, post-streptococcal GN, IgA/HSP, MPGN |\n| **Type 3** | PAUCI-IMMUNE (absent) | ANCA vasculitis: GPA (c-ANCA), MPA & EGPA (p-ANCA) |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — RPGN by IF: Line / Lumps / Lacking**\n\nLINEAR = anti-GBM (type 1).\n\nLUMPY-bumpy granular = immune complex (type 2).\n\nLACKING / pauci-immune = ANCA vasculitis (type 3).\n\n**Treatment  **Treat the underlying cause urgently — high-dose steroids + immunosuppression (cyclophosphamide) ± PLASMA EXCHANGE (especially anti-GBM and severe ANCA). Dialysis if kidney failure. Speed matters — delay = permanent loss.\n\n**Q25:  ****Goodpasture syndrome: how would they present in the ER?**\n\n**What it is  **Anti-GBM disease (RPGN type 1, LINEAR IF) WITH lung involvement. Autoantibodies attack type-IV collagen, which sits in BOTH the glomerular basement membrane AND the alveolar basement membrane.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Goodpasture = lungs + kidneys bleed together**\n\nAnti-GBM disease ALONE = kidney only (haematuria, AKI, NO haemoptysis).\n\nAnti-GBM + HAEMOPTYSIS = GOODPASTURE (collagen IV is shared by lung + kidney).\n\nAssociated with HLA-DR2.\n\n### **ER presentation (what they actually look like)**\n\n- Respiratory: HAEMOPTYSIS (coughing blood), dyspnoea, possibly hypoxia from pulmonary haemorrhage — may look like a chest emergency.\n\n- Renal: cola-coloured urine (haematuria), oliguria, rapidly rising creatinine, hypertension — a rapidly progressive nephritic picture.\n\n- Often a young man, sometimes after a respiratory trigger (smoking, infection).\n\n**Diagnosis  **Anti-GBM antibodies in serum; LINEAR IgG on IF; crescents on biopsy. Chest imaging shows pulmonary infiltrates/haemorrhage.\n\n**Treatment  **URGENT: plasma exchange (removes the antibody) + high-dose steroids + cyclophosphamide. The lung haemorrhage can be life-threatening, so this is treated as an emergency.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — ER one-liner**\n\nYoung patient + haemoptysis + cola-coloured urine + rising creatinine = Goodpasture until proven otherwise. Send anti-GBM, start plasma exchange — don't wait.\n\n# **PART 5 · ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY ****&**** ELECTROLYTES**\n\n**Q26:  ****Differential diagnosis: AKI vs CKD**\n\n| **Feature** | **AKI** | **CKD** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Onset** | Hours–days–weeks | Months–years |\n| **Reversibility** | Often reversible (no structural change yet) | Largely irreversible (structural scarring) |\n| **Kidney size on US** | Normal or enlarged | SMALL, shrunken (except PKD, diabetes, amyloid) |\n| **Anaemia** | Usually absent (acute) | Present (chronic ↓EPO) |\n| **Bone disease (↑PTH)** | Absent | Present (secondary hyperparathyroidism) |\n| **Baseline bloods** | Previously normal creatinine | Long-standing raised creatinine |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — CKD clues = the 'chronic' fingerprints**\n\nSMALL kidneys + ANAEMIA + high PTH/bone disease = chronic (the body had time to adapt). An acute rise on a previously normal creatinine with normal-sized kidneys and no anaemia = AKI.\n\n**⭐  Note: AKI and CKD feed each other — AKI causes/accelerates CKD, and CKD makes AKI more likely. Both raise cardiovascular risk.**\n\n**Q27:  ****Exact definition of AKI · what counts as 'acute'**\n\n**Definition  **AKI = an abrupt drop in kidney filtration/excretory function over DAYS TO WEEKS → retention of nitrogenous + waste products. It is a CLINICAL diagnosis (no structural kidney damage required).\n\n- **Hallmark: abrupt FALL in urine output + abrupt RISE in serum creatinine.**\n\n- 'Acute' timeframe = onset within 2–48 hours (progressive = 1–2 months; chronic = months–years).\n\n**KDIGO staging (how it's graded)  **Based on the size of creatinine rise (absolute daily rise OR the weekly multiplier of baseline) AND urine output (which indirectly reflects GFR). Mortality up to 50%; 2–5% of all patients develop AKI.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — AKI = sudden + reversible-if-caught**\n\nThe whole point of catching AKI early: there are NO permanent anatomical changes yet, so the goal is to RECOVER full function and prevent slide into ESRD. (Contrast CKD, where the goal is just to SLOW progression.)\n\n**Q28:  ****Causes of AKI: pre-renal / intrarenal / post-renal (+ prostate, US, sex-specific)**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — The 3 'P-locations' of AKI**\n\nPRE-renal (70%) = problem BEFORE the kidney → not enough blood reaches it.\n\nINTRA-renal (10%) = problem INSIDE the kidney tissue.\n\nPOST-renal (20%) = problem AFTER the kidney → blocked outflow.\n\n### **PRE-RENAL (70%) — low perfusion, kidney itself is healthy**\n\n- Low effective blood volume: heart failure (cardiorenal syndrome), liver failure (hepatorenal syndrome), hypovolaemia (vomiting/diarrhoea/bleeding/diuresis), hypotension/shock.\n\n- Arteriolar problems: afferent VASOCONSTRICTION (NSAIDs, tacrolimus — block protective PGI₂) OR efferent VASODILATION (ACEi/ARB) → both drop GFR.\n\n- Renal artery stenosis (atherosclerosis if >50y; fibromuscular dysplasia if <50y).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Pre-renal exam answers**\n\nMost common pre-renal cause = HYPOVOLAEMIA (volume depletion).\n\nKidney on US in pre-renal = NORMAL (the tissue is fine; it's just under-perfused).\n\nDanger combo: NSAID + ACEi/ARB in a volume-depleted or CKD patient → both arterioles hit → pre-renal AKI.\n\n### **INTRA-RENAL (10%) — the tissue is damaged**\n\n- Tubular: ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (commonest intra-renal) — ischaemic (untreated pre-renal) or nephrotoxic (vancomycin, aminoglycosides, contrast; pigments myoglobin/haemoglobin; uric acid; Bence-Jones). Hallmark: muddy-brown granular casts.\n\n- Interstitial: acute interstitial nephritis (NSAIDs, beta-lactams, PPIs) → fever + rash + eosinophils + WBC casts.\n\n- Glomerular: glomerulonephritis (ANCA, anti-GBM, lupus) → RBC casts + proteinuria. Vascular: thrombotic microangiopathy (HUS/TTP).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Intra-renal exam answer**\n\nMost common intra-renal cause = ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (ATN), recognised by muddy-brown granular casts.\n\n### **POST-RENAL (20%) — obstruction of outflow**\n\n- Backflow of urine → raised pressure back to the glomerulus → filtration stops → hydronephrosis on US.\n\n- Proximal obstruction (must be BILATERAL or a solitary kidney to cause AKI): stones, urinary tract or pelvic cancer.\n\n- Distal obstruction: PROSTATIC HYPERTROPHY → incomplete voiding (pollakiuria) → eventually complete blockage → anuria + AKI + bladder distension.\n\n**🔑  How to diagnose prostatic hypertrophy (asked specifically)**\n\nDigital rectal examination (feel an enlarged prostate), OR\n\nAbdominal/bladder ULTRASOUND showing a distended bladder that fails to empty (large post-void residual) + bilateral hydronephrosis.\n\nBladder distension is the feature that separates DISTAL (bladder full) from PROXIMAL (bladder not distended) obstruction.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Sex-specific causes (asked)**\n\nMales: prostatic hypertrophy / prostate cancer (distal obstruction).\n\nFemales: tumours of the female genital tract compressing the ureters.\n\n**Q29:  ****Hyponatraemia and hypernatraemia**\n\n**Core idea  **Sodium problems are usually WATER problems. Hyponatraemia = too much water relative to sodium; hypernatraemia = too little water relative to sodium.\n\n### **HYPONATRAEMIA (Na ****<**** 135) — the 3-step approach**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Step 1 OSMOLALITY → Step 2 SEVERITY → Step 3 URINE → Step 4 VOLUME**\n\nStep 1 — serum osmolality: HIGH (>300, pseudo — e.g. hyperglycaemia/mannitol pulls water out) or NORMAL (280–300, pseudo — lipids/protein take up serum space). Only LOW osmolality (<280) = TRUE hyponatraemia.\n\nStep 2 — severity: if Na <120 or acute (<48h) → 3% hypertonic saline. Otherwise fluid-restrict while you work it up.\n\nStep 3 — urine osmolality: >300 = ADH-dependent; <300 = ADH-suppressed (water overload).\n\nStep 4 — volume status on exam: hypovolaemic / euvolaemic / hypervolaemic.\n\n| **Volume type** | **Mechanism** | **Examples** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hypovolaemic** | Lose Na > water | Diuretics (thiazide worst), vomiting/diarrhoea, burns, Addison's, cerebral salt wasting |\n| **Euvolaemic** | Water retention dilutes Na | SIADH (CNS injury, lung disease, small-cell lung ca, SSRIs, carbamazepine); also tea-and-toast / beer potomania (ADH-suppressed) |\n| **Hypervolaemic** | Volume overload + dilution | Heart failure, cirrhosis (urine osm >300), CKD stage 4/5 (urine osm <300) |\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Two killer complications**\n\nAcute severe hyponatraemia → CEREBRAL OEDEMA (water floods the brain) → headache, vomiting, seizures.\n\nOver-correcting too fast (>6–8 mEq/L/day) → OSMOTIC DEMYELINATION (central pontine myelinolysis) → quadriplegia, dysarthria, dysphagia 1–2 weeks later. So correct SLOWLY (max ~6 mEq/L/day).\n\n### **HYPERNATRAEMIA (Na ****>**** 145) — too little water**\n\n- Don't bother checking serum osmolality (always hypertonic — no 'pseudo' form). Classify by volume:\n\n| **Volume type** | **Mechanism / causes** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Hypovolaemic** | Water loss > Na loss: GI (diarrhoea/vomiting), skin (burns/sweat), osmotic diuresis, OR no access to water (intubated, altered mental status, elderly) |\n| **Euvolaemic** | DIABETES INSIPIDUS → polyuria. Central DI (no ADH made: trauma/surgery) vs Nephrogenic DI (kidney ignores ADH: lithium, low K, high Ca) |\n| **Hypervolaemic** | Hyperaldosteronism (Na + water retained) → hypertensive + hypokalaemic; or iatrogenic hypertonic saline |\n\n**💡  DDAVP (desmopressin) challenge separates the DI types: in CENTRAL DI urine concentrates (the kidney CAN respond), in NEPHROGENIC DI it does not.**\n\n**Q30:  ****Hyperkalaemia: signs, symptoms, major effects**\n\n**Definition  **K⁺ > 5.3 mEq/L (normal 3.5–5). Causes: shift out of cells (acidosis, low insulin, beta-blockers, cell lysis — rhabdo/tumour lysis/haemolysis) OR retention (↓GFR in AKI/CKD, hypoaldosteronism, ACEi/ARB, K-sparing diuretics).\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — 'MURDER' — effects of hyperkalaemia**\n\nM = Muscle weakness\n\nU = Urine output decreased (oliguria/anuria)\n\nR = Respiratory distress (diaphragm is muscle)\n\nD = Decreased cardiac contractility\n\nE = ECG changes\n\nR = Reflex abnormalities (areflexia)\n\n### **The ECG progression (the dangerous part — learn the order + the K levels)**\n\n| **K⁺ level** | **ECG change** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **6–8 mEq/L** | PEAKED T waves (the earliest sign) |\n| **~ 8** | Long PR → loss of P waves; widening QRS |\n| **~ 10** | ST depression, very wide QRS |\n| **>**** 10** | SINE WAVE → V-fib → asystole / cardiac arrest |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Hyperkalaemia ECG: 'Peak → Lose P → Widen → Sine → Stop'**\n\nTall peaked T → flat/absent P → wide QRS → sine wave → cardiac arrest. The higher the K, the further down this ladder.\n\n### **Treatment (in order, if ECG changes)**\n\n- **CALCIUM gluconate — stabilises the cardiac membrane (doesn't lower K, just protects the heart). FIRST if ECG changes.**\n\n- Insulin (+ glucose) + inhaled salbutamol — SHIFT K⁺ into cells (stimulate Na/K-ATPase).\n\n- Sodium bicarbonate if acidotic — also shifts K in.\n\n- Then REMOVE K from the body: K-binding resin (mild/anuric) or loop diuretic (moderate/oliguric); haemodialysis if severe/refractory.\n\n**⚠️  Calcium gluconate PROTECTS the heart but does NOT lower potassium — you still must shift it in and then remove it. Forgetting the 'remove' step is a classic error.**\n\n**Q31:  ****Hepatorenal syndrome (HRS)**\n\n**What it is  **Kidney failure CAUSED BY advanced liver disease (decompensated cirrhosis) — the kidneys themselves are structurally normal. It is a form of PRE-RENAL AKI.\n\n**🔑  Mechanism — why a sick liver starves the kidney**\n\nCirrhosis → portal hypertension → the body releases vasodilators → SPLANCHNIC (gut) VASODILATION → blood pools in the gut → effective arterial volume reaching the kidney falls.\n\nCirrhosis also → low albumin → low oncotic pressure → more fluid leaves the vessels (ascites) → even less circulating volume.\n\nThe kidney senses low perfusion → activates RAAS → renal vasoconstriction → GFR drops → AKI. The kidney is fine; it's been 'switched off' by the circulation.\n\n**Picture  **Cirrhotic patient (ascites, oedema) with rising creatinine + oliguria + very LOW urine sodium (kidney clinging to salt), with no other cause of AKI.\n\n**Treatment  **Albumin (raises oncotic pressure / volume) + a splanchnic vasoconstrictor (terlipressin/octreotide ± midodrine) to redirect blood back to the kidney. Definitive cure = liver transplant.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — HRS = the kidney is an innocent bystander**\n\nTreat the CIRCULATION, not the kidney: fill the tank (albumin) and squeeze the leaking gut vessels (vasoconstrictor). Mirror of cardiorenal syndrome, where the HEART starves the kidney.\n\n**Q32:  ****Acute interstitial nephritis (AIN)**\n\n**What it is  **An intra-renal AKI from inflammation of the kidney's interstitium — usually an allergic (type-IV hypersensitivity) reaction to a DRUG.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Causes: 'the 3 P-ish drugs' — Pee-pills, Penicillins, PPIs (+ NSAIDs)**\n\nAntibiotics (beta-lactams/penicillins, sulfa), NSAIDs, PPIs, diuretics — the classic culprits.\n\n### **Classic triad + labs**\n\n- Triad: FEVER + RASH + EOSINOPHILIA (though all three together are uncommon in practice).\n\n- Urine: WBC casts + eosinophils (eosinophiluria); mild tubular proteinuria.\n\n- History: started a new drug days–weeks before AKI.\n\n**Treatment  **STOP the offending drug (often enough to recover) ± steroids if severe/slow to resolve.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — AIN vs ATN**\n\nAIN = allergic, drug-triggered, fever+rash+EOSINOPHILS, WBC casts. ATN = ischaemic/toxic, MUDDY-BROWN granular casts, no allergy features. Both are intra-renal but the casts give it away.\n\n**Q33:  ****Oliguria, anuria, polyuria, pollakiuria — definitions ****&**** the % with oliguria/anuria**\n\n**Normal urine output  **1.5–2 L/day.\n\n| **Term** | **Definition** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Oliguria** | Urine output < 400 mL/day |\n| **Anuria** | Urine output < 100 mL/day |\n| **Polyuria** | Urine output > 2.5–3 L/day |\n| **Pollakiuria** | Abnormally FREQUENT urination (small volumes, often) — a frequency problem, not a total-volume problem |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Volume vs frequency**\n\nOliguria/anuria/polyuria are about TOTAL VOLUME per day (little / almost none / lots).\n\nPollakiuria is about FREQUENCY (peeing often, small amounts) — think prostate/bladder irritation, early outflow obstruction, or UTI.\n\n**The '%' point  **In AKI you can see oliguria/anuria, but a substantial proportion of AKI is actually NON-oliguric (urine output preserved) — so normal urine output does NOT exclude AKI. Always check creatinine, not just the urine volume.\n\n# **PART 6 · BLOOD PRESSURE, UTI ****&**** STONES**\n\n**Q34:  ****Main mechanisms regulating blood pressure**\n\nGroup them by SPEED — examiners like 'fast, medium, slow':\n\n| **Speed** | **Mechanism** | **How it works** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Seconds** | Baroreceptor reflex | Carotid/aortic baroreceptors sense BP → autonomic nervous system adjusts heart rate + vessel tone instantly |\n| **Minutes** | Sympathetic / catecholamines | Adrenaline/noradrenaline → vasoconstriction + ↑heart rate/contractility |\n| **Minutes–hours** | RAAS | ↓renal perfusion → renin → angiotensin II (vasoconstriction) + aldosterone (Na/water retention) → ↑volume + ↑BP |\n| **Hours** | ADH (vasopressin) | Water reabsorption in collecting duct → ↑volume; high levels also vasoconstrict |\n| **Days (the master control)** | Renal pressure-natriuresis | The KIDNEY sets long-term BP by adjusting Na/water excretion — high BP → dump Na/water → volume falls → BP falls |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — BP control = 'Bears Sometimes Run And Run' (fast → slow)**\n\nBaroreceptors (seconds) → Sympathetic (minutes) → RAAS (minutes–hours) → ADH (hours) → Renal Na/water handling (days, the long-term boss).\n\nThe KIDNEY has the final say long-term — which is exactly why kidney disease so reliably causes hypertension.\n\n**⭐  Tie back to nephrology: the kidney is the long-term BP controller (via RAAS + Na/water handling), so any kidney disease dysregulates BP — and high BP then damages the kidney further (the vicious cycle from Q1/Q6).**\n\n**Q35:  ****Diagnosis of pyelonephritis: signs, symptoms, ultrasound**\n\n**What it is  **Infection of the kidney itself (upper UTI) — the renal pelvis + parenchyma.\n\n### **Signs ****&**** symptoms**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Pyelonephritis = lower-UTI symptoms PLUS 'the kidney is angry'**\n\nLower-tract symptoms: dysuria, frequency, urgency.\n\nPLUS systemic/upper signs: FEVER + chills/rigors, FLANK PAIN, nausea/vomiting.\n\nKey SIGN: positive GIORDANO sign (costovertebral-angle tenderness — fist percussion over the kidney angle hurts).\n\n**Labs/urine  **Urinalysis: WBCs + WBC casts (casts = it's in the KIDNEY, not just the bladder) + nitrites + leukocyte esterase + bacteria. Urine culture confirms organism.\n\n### **Ultrasound — when and what**\n\n- US is used to look for COMPLICATIONS / obstruction, not to diagnose simple pyelonephritis.\n\n- Findings: enlarged kidney with hyper-echoic zones; focal lesions or material inside enlarged calyces (vs hydronephrosis, where you'd see clear urine without material).\n\n- Crucial: exclude an obstructing stone with hydronephrosis (an obstructed, infected kidney = pyonephrosis = emergency needing drainage).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Giordano vs Blumberg (don't mix them up)**\n\nGIORDANO sign = fist percussion at the costovertebral angle → kidney pain → pyelonephritis or stones.\n\nBLUMBERG sign = rebound tenderness on the abdomen → peritonitis. Different test, different problem.\n\n**Q36:  ****UTI: upper vs lower, which is more severe, why hard to treat, link to stones**\n\n|  | **LOWER UTI** | **UPPER UTI** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Site** | Bladder (cystitis), urethra | Kidney (pyelonephritis) |\n| **Symptoms** | Dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain — NO fever usually | Fever, flank pain, rigors, vomiting + lower symptoms |\n| **Severity** | Milder | MORE SEVERE — can cause sepsis, scarring, AKI |\n\n### **Why UTIs can be hard to treat with antibiotics**\n\n- Biofilms (e.g. on stones or catheters) shield bacteria from antibiotics.\n\n- Rising antibiotic RESISTANCE among uropathogens (E. coli, etc.).\n\n- Incomplete bladder emptying / obstruction leaves a reservoir of bacteria (stasis = re-infection).\n\n- Some antibiotics don't reach therapeutic concentration in kidney tissue or behind an obstruction.\n\n**🔑  Is nephrolithiasis associated with UTI? — YES, both ways**\n\nStones cause UTIs: a stone obstructs flow → urinary STASIS → bacteria multiply → recurrent/hard-to-clear infection. You often can't sterilise the urine until the stone is removed.\n\nUTIs cause stones: urease-positive bacteria (Proteus, Klebsiella) split urea → raise urine pH → form STRUVITE ('coffin-lid', infection) stones. A vicious cycle.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Asymptomatic bacteriuria rule**\n\nBacteria in urine WITHOUT WBCs/RBCs/symptoms → do NOT give antibiotics — EXCEPT in PREGNANCY (where you do treat). Urine is normally sterile (acidic); only TB tolerates it.\n\n**Q37:  ****Nephrolithiasis: risks and complications**\n\n**What it is  **Kidney stones — crystals that aggregate in the urinary tract. Present with colicky FLANK pain radiating to the groin + haematuria ± nausea.\n\n### **Risk factors**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Stone risks = 'Dehydration + Diet + Drainage problems + Disease'**\n\nDehydration / low fluid intake (concentrated urine = crystals form).\n\nDiet: high oxalate (→ calcium oxalate), high purine (→ uric acid stones in gout, low-pH urine), high salt.\n\nDrainage/stasis: obstruction, anatomical abnormality.\n\nDisease/infection: urease-positive bacteria → struvite stones; hyperparathyroidism (high calcium); gout (uric acid).\n\n**Stone types by urine pH (from the sediment notes)  **LOW pH: uric acid (hexagonal), calcium oxalate (Maltese cross). HIGH pH: calcium phosphate (wedge), struvite (coffin-lid, urease bacteria), cystine.\n\n### **Complications**\n\n- OBSTRUCTION → hydronephrosis → POST-RENAL AKI (especially if bilateral or a solitary kidney).\n\n- INFECTION behind the stone → pyelonephritis → potentially pyonephrosis/sepsis (an emergency).\n\n- Recurrent stones → chronic damage → CKD over time.\n\n- Severe pain (renal colic) — one of the worst pains in medicine.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Why bilateral matters for AKI**\n\nA single stone in one ureter rarely causes AKI (the other kidney compensates). AKI from stones requires BILATERAL obstruction OR a stone in a SOLITARY/only-functioning kidney. Examiners test this.\n\n# **PART 7 · DIABETIC NEPHROPATHY · RAS · PKD**\n\n**Q38:  ****Diabetic nephropathy: normal proteinuria, progression, risk factors, monitoring**\n\n**Why it matters  **The #1 cause of CKD (25%) and of ESRD (50%).\n\n**🔑  Mechanism — how high sugar scleroses the glomerulus**\n\nGlucose sticks to proteins without enzymes (non-enzymatic glycation) → glycoproteins deposit in the EFFERENT arteriole → its lumen narrows → pressure builds up INSIDE the glomerulus (intraglomerular hypertension) → an early phase of HYPERFILTRATION (GFR actually HIGH at first).\n\nHyperglycaemia also upregulates SGLT2 → more Na reabsorbed in PCT → low salt at macula densa → fooled into activating RAAS → more efferent constriction → more pressure.\n\nMesangial cells can't tolerate the high pressure → they lay down extracellular matrix → GLOMERULOSCLEROSIS → nephron loss.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Diabetic nephropathy progression: 'High GFR fools you, then it crashes'**\n\nStage 1: HYPERfiltration (GFR high) — looks fine, but damage is starting.\n\nStage 2: microalbuminuria (30–300 mg/day) — the FIRST detectable sign.\n\nStage 3: overt proteinuria (>300 mg/day) + falling GFR.\n\nStage 4: declining GFR → Stage 5: ESRD.\n\n**Normal proteinuria / the thresholds  **Normal <30 mg/day (ACR <30). Microalbuminuria = 30–300 mg/day (the earliest, still-reversible sign). Overt nephropathy = >300 mg/day.\n\n**Pathology to name  **Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (nodular glomerulosclerosis), thick GBM, arteriolar hyalinosis, tubulointerstitial fibrosis.\n\n### **Monitoring ****&**** prevention**\n\n- Screen for microalbuminuria ANNUALLY — from diagnosis in T2 diabetes; from 5 years after diagnosis in T1.\n\n- ACE inhibitors / ARBs prevent progression by relieving intraglomerular pressure (dilate the efferent arteriole) — start them early.\n\n- Tight control of glucose + BP + proteinuria. BP target <130/80 (or <125/75 if proteinuria >1 g/day).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — The retinopathy shortcut**\n\nRetinal and renal vessels behave alike. In T2 diabetes, retinopathy almost always means nephropathy too — so you can diagnose diabetic nephropathy WITHOUT biopsy. If there's NO retinopathy in a diabetic with proteinuria, biopsy to look for another cause. (In T1 they're not linked.)\n\n**Q39:  ****Normal creatinine levels · unilateral renal artery stenosis**\n\n**Normal creatinine  **Men 0.7–1.2 mg/dL; women 0.5–1.0 mg/dL. Adjust up for muscular patients, down in pregnancy. Creatinine only rises once GFR has fallen ~50% (insensitive early).\n\n### **Renal artery stenosis (RAS)**\n\n- Cause: atherosclerosis if >50 years; fibromuscular dysplasia if <50 years (classically young women).\n\n**🔑  Why UNILATERAL RAS causes hypertension (and why ACEi is risky here)**\n\nOne narrowed renal artery → that kidney senses low perfusion → pumps out renin → systemic RAAS activation → hypertension (often resistant). The OTHER kidney is fine and handles fluid, so creatinine can stay near-normal — making it sneaky.\n\nGiving an ACEi/ARB removes the angiotensin-II that was constricting the efferent arteriole of the affected kidney → its GFR collapses → creatinine jumps. In BILATERAL RAS this can cause AKI — which is why a big creatinine rise after starting an ACEi should make you suspect RAS.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — RAS clue**\n\nResistant hypertension + a creatinine that SPIKES when you start an ACEi/ARB = renal artery stenosis until proven otherwise. Young woman → fibromuscular dysplasia; older arteriopath → atherosclerosis.\n\n**Treatment  **Control BP; endovascular revascularisation (angioplasty/stent) for selected cases, especially bilateral disease causing AKI or refractory hypertension.\n\n**Q40:  ****Polycystic kidney disease: why remove sick kidneys at transplant · other organs involved**\n\n**What it is  **Autosomal DOMINANT PKD — the 4th commonest cause of ESRD. Mutations in PKD1 (polycystin-1, worse) or PKD2 (polycystin-2, milder). Cilia dysfunction → uncontrolled tubular cell proliferation → fluid-filled cysts grow in cortex + medulla → kidneys become huge (6–8 kg).\n\n**🔑  Why remove the native kidneys before/at transplant?**\n\nThe diseased PKD kidneys are MASSIVE (up to 6–8 kg) — there's physically no room for the new kidney, and they compress other organs.\n\nThey keep causing problems: recurrent cyst infections, bleeding, pain, and a risk of malignant degeneration.\n\nSo binephrectomy makes space + removes the source of infection/bleeding/cancer risk. (In most OTHER renal diseases the small native kidneys are LEFT in place — PKD is the exception because they're enormous.)\n\n### **Other organs involved (it's a systemic ciliopathy/connective-tissue disease)**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — PKD outside the kidney: 'Berry, Liver, Valve, Gut'**\n\nBERRY ANEURYSMS in the brain → risk of subarachnoid haemorrhage / sudden death (screen if family history).\n\nLIVER cysts (and pancreatic cysts).\n\nMitral VALVE prolapse (weak connective tissue / chordae).\n\nGUT: colonic diverticulosis.\n\n**Clinical features  **Flank pain (stretched capsule), gross haematuria (cyst rupture — NO casts), hypertension (often the first sign — cysts compress vessels → RAAS), recurrent UTIs/stones from stasis. Progresses to CKD as cysts crush functional nephrons.\n\n**Diagnosis/prognosis  **US/CT/MRI (don't screen <15y — adult onset). Prognosis worse with PKD1, larger total kidney volume, faster ΔTKV/year. Note: because cysts make EPO, PKD patients may have BETTER Hb than expected for their GFR.\n\n# **PART 8 · CLINICAL CASES (Q41–Q44)**\n\nThese test whether you can APPLY everything. Approach each as: stabilise → localise (pre/intra/post) → investigate → treat.\n\n**Q41:  ****Patient with increased serum creatinine — how do you work it up?**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Raised creatinine → answer in this order**\n\n1) Is it ACUTE or CHRONIC? Check old creatinine values + kidney size on US (small = chronic) + anaemia/PTH (present = chronic).\n\n2) If acute → localise: PRE-renal (volume/perfusion?), INTRA-renal (casts? drugs?), POST-renal (US for hydronephrosis/full bladder?).\n\n3) Urinalysis + sediment (RBC casts → nephritic; muddy brown → ATN; WBC casts → AIN/pyelo; proteinuria amount).\n\n4) Bloods: electrolytes (K!), bicarbonate, calcium/phosphate, FBC, and review the drug chart for nephrotoxins.\n\n5) US first-line imaging (size, obstruction). Biopsy if cause unclear / nephritic / nephrotic adult.\n\n**⭐  First-line imaging is ALWAYS ultrasound: it instantly separates obstruction (hydronephrosis → post-renal) from small chronic kidneys (CKD) from normal-sized kidneys (acute, pre/intra-renal).**\n\n**Q42:  ****82-year-old, no urine for 12–15 hours — what do you assess first and how?**\n\n**🔑  Anuria in an elderly person = obstruction until proven otherwise**\n\nFIRST assess the BLADDER — palpate + bladder ultrasound (bladder scan). Anuria with a DISTENDED bladder = the outflow is blocked (in an 82-year-old man, almost always PROSTATIC obstruction).\n\nWhy bladder first? Because it's the fastest, most reversible cause: a distended obstructed bladder is relieved instantly with a FOLEY CATHETER → urine pours out → AKI reverses. You don't want to miss a fixable mechanical block.\n\n### **The logic tree**\n\n- Bladder DISTENDED → distal obstruction (prostate) → catheterise → relief.\n\n- Bladder EMPTY → check upstream: US for bilateral hydronephrosis (proximal obstruction) → then think pre-renal (volume/perfusion, cardiovascular) and intra-renal.\n\n- Also rapidly assess: vitals (shock?), volume status, K⁺ + ECG (anuria → hyperkalaemia risk), and the drug chart.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Anuric elderly patient = 'Feel the bladder, then scan it'**\n\nFull bladder → catheter (post-renal, instant fix). Empty bladder → ultrasound the kidneys for hydronephrosis, then work pre-renal/intra-renal. Don't forget to check potassium — anuria kills via hyperkalaemia.\n\n**Q43:  ****16-year-old: haematuria for a few days, hypertension, petechiae**\n\n**Pattern recognition  **Young patient + nephritic picture (haematuria + hypertension) + PETECHIAE (skin purpura) → think a systemic small-vessel process hitting skin AND kidney.\n\n### **Top differentials**\n\n- **IgA VASCULITIS (Henoch-Schönlein purpura): the classic answer — palpable purpura (often buttocks/legs) + IgA nephropathy + arthralgia + abdominal pain, often after a recent infection. Same galactose-deficient IgA as IgA nephropathy.**\n\n- Other vasculitides / RPGN (ANCA-associated) if more severe.\n\n- Consider haematological causes of petechiae (thrombocytopenia, e.g. HUS/TTP with AKI) — check platelets + smear.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Skin purpura + nephritic kidney in a teen = IgA vasculitis (HSP)**\n\nRecent infection → IgA immune complexes deposit in skin (purpura), gut (pain), joints (arthralgia) AND kidney (haematuria + IgA nephropathy). Normal/high C3, raised IgA.\n\n**Work-up  **BP, urinalysis (RBC casts, proteinuria), bloods (creatinine, FBC + platelets, complement C3/C4, IgA), ANCA/anti-GBM if severe, and skin/renal biopsy if needed. Most childhood HSP nephritis is self-limiting; treat BP with ACEi, immunosuppression if severe.\n\n**Q44:  ****70-year-old, cardiac history, CKD4 (GFR 20), approaching ESRD — counteract each RAAS effect**\n\n**The scenario  **Low GFR → the kidney over-activates RAAS, producing four harmful effects. The question wants you to name each effect and how to counteract it.\n\n| **RAAS effect** | **Why it's harmful here** | **How to counteract it** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Renin release** | Drives the whole cascade | ACE inhibitor / ARB (block angiotensin II generation/action) — also renoprotective |\n| **Angiotensin II (vasoconstriction)** | ↑afterload → strains a failing heart; ↑intraglomerular pressure | ACEi/ARB → vasodilation, lower afterload, lower intraglomerular pressure |\n| **Aldosterone → Na retention** | Fluid overload → oedema, ↑BP, heart strain | Salt restriction + loop diuretic; aldosterone antagonist (cautiously — hyperkalaemia risk in CKD4) |\n| **Sympathetic activation** | ↑heart rate/afterload, arrhythmia risk | Beta-blocker (helps the cardiac history too) |\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — The CKD4 caveat**\n\nACEi/ARB and aldosterone antagonists all RAISE potassium — and this patient already can't excrete K⁺ at GFR 20. So you must monitor potassium closely and accept that some agents are used cautiously or in lower doses. Balancing heart protection against hyperkalaemia is the whole art of this question.\n\n**⭐  Framing answer: 'Low GFR over-activates RAAS, which protects perfusion short-term but harms a failing heart and the kidney long-term — so I block it at each level (ACEi/ARB for renin/AT-II, diuretic/salt-restriction ± aldosterone antagonist for aldosterone, beta-blocker for sympathetic drive), while watching potassium because he's CKD4.'**\n\n# **PART 9 · HIGH-YIELD TOPICS (now at full depth)**\n\n**These DO get examined. Each is built up the same way as the main questions — mechanism, presentation, diagnosis, treatment, memory trick — so any follow-up is coverable.**\n\n## **Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)**\n\n**What it is  **Death of tubular cells → the commonest cause of intra-renal AKI. Two flavours: ischaemic and nephrotoxic.\n\n**🔑  Mechanism**\n\nIschaemic ATN: prolonged low perfusion (untreated pre-renal AKI) starves the tubules — the S3 segment of the PCT and the thick ascending limb are most vulnerable (highest oxygen demand).\n\nNephrotoxic ATN: toxins poison the tubular cells directly — drugs (aminoglycosides, vancomycin, amphotericin, contrast), pigments (myoglobin, haemoglobin), crystals (uric acid), light chains (Bence-Jones).\n\nDead cells slough off → form muddy-brown granular casts → can plug the tubule → further drops GFR.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — ATN = muddy-brown casts + the 3 phases**\n\nHallmark: MUDDY-BROWN GRANULAR CASTS.\n\nPhases: INITIATION (injury) → MAINTENANCE (oliguria, the dangerous phase — hyperkalaemia/acidosis) → RECOVERY (polyuria as tubules regenerate, watch for low K now).\n\n**Treatment  **Supportive: remove the cause, stop nephrotoxins, IV fluids to let tubules heal, manage K/acidosis, dialysis if needed. Usually reversible if the patient survives the maintenance phase.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — A damaged S3-PCT loses the renin brake**\n\nDamage to the PCT S3 segment → less Na/Cl reabsorbed → HIGH salt at the macula densa → renin is SUPPRESSED → the kidney can no longer defend its own perfusion in hypotension. A nasty self-worsening loop.\n\n## **Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA)**\n\n**What it is  **A NORMAL-anion-gap metabolic acidosis caused by the tubule failing to handle acid/bicarbonate. Three types — the trick is the URINE pH and the POTASSIUM.\n\n| **Type** | **Defect** | **Blood K⁺** | **Urine pH** | **Classic causes** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Type 1 (Distal)** | DCT can't secrete H⁺ | LOW | HIGH (>5.5) | Autoimmune (Sjögren, SLE); stones |\n| **Type 2 (Proximal)** | PCT can't reabsorb HCO₃⁻ | LOW | LOW (<5.5)* | Fanconi syndrome, acetazolamide, myeloma |\n| **Type 4 (Hyperkalaemic)** | Aldosterone deficiency/resistance | HIGH | LOW (<5.5) | Diabetes, NSAIDs, ACEi, heparin (the commonest) |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — RTA: the 1-2-4 ladder by K and urine pH**\n\nType 1 (distal) = can't make urine acidic → urine pH HIGH, blood K LOW. (Think 'distal = high urine pH'.)\n\nType 2 (proximal) = wastes bicarb until threshold, then urine turns acidic → urine pH LOW, blood K LOW.\n\nType 4 (the odd one) = HYPERkalaemic (aldosterone problem) → urine pH LOW. The only RTA with HIGH potassium.\n\n*Type 2 urine is paradoxically acidic once the lowered HCO₃ threshold is reached (the DCT still works).\n\n**Why Type 1 makes stones  **Persistently alkaline urine + low citrate → calcium phosphate precipitates → kidney stones + nephrocalcinosis. (Distal RTA is the stone-forming one.)\n\n**Treatment  **Bicarbonate replacement (and K replacement in types 1/2). Type 4: low-K diet, fludrocortisone, stop the offending drug.\n\n## **Contrast-Induced Nephropathy (CIN)**\n\n**What it is  **AKI within 48–72h of IV iodinated contrast — a form of nephrotoxic ATN (direct tubular toxicity + medullary vasoconstriction/ischaemia).\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — CIN risk factors: 'old, dry, diabetic, dosed, on nephrotoxins'**\n\nPre-existing CKD (especially GFR <30 — biggest risk), diabetes, age >75, dehydration/volume depletion, high contrast volume, concurrent nephrotoxins (NSAIDs).\n\n**🔑  Prevention (mostly what's testable)**\n\nIV HYDRATION with 0.9% saline before AND after contrast (the single best preventive measure).\n\nUse the lowest contrast volume; stop nephrotoxic drugs around the time of the scan.\n\nConsider an alternative that avoids iodinated contrast (US, or MRI WITHOUT gadolinium if GFR allows).\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Two contrast rules tied to CKD stage**\n\nPast Stage 3 (GFR <60): avoid IODINATED contrast → risk of contrast nephropathy.\n\nPast Stage 4 (GFR <30): avoid GADOLINIUM → risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis. (Different agent, different complication — examiners love this pair.)\n\n## **Rhabdomyolysis**\n\n**What it is  **Massive muscle breakdown → myoglobin floods the blood → myoglobin is nephrotoxic → pigment-induced ATN/AKI.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Rhabdo causes: 'Crush, Crash, Convulse, Cocaine, Statins'**\n\nCrush injury / prolonged immobility (elderly 'long lie'), seizures, extreme exercise, drugs (statins, cocaine), some toxins/infections.\n\n### **How it injures the kidney + the labs**\n\n- Myoglobin is directly toxic to tubules + precipitates in (acidic) urine → tubular obstruction → ATN.\n\n- Labs: CK markedly raised (often >5000, sometimes >>1000 is the clue), myoglobin in serum AND urine.\n\n- Electrolytes: HYPERkalaemia + HYPERphosphataemia (from lysed muscle) + early HYPOcalcaemia (Ca binds released phosphate), then HYPERcalcaemia in recovery.\n\n- Urine dipstick: positive for 'blood' but NO RBCs on microscopy (the dipstick is detecting myoglobin) = a pseudohaematuria clue.\n\n**Treatment  **Aggressive IV fluids (flush the tubules, dilute myoglobin) ± urine alkalinisation; treat hyperkalaemia; dialysis if severe.\n\n## **Tumour Lysis Syndrome (TLS)**\n\n**What it is  **Massive cancer-cell death (often just after starting chemo for a bulky, fast-growing tumour, e.g. lymphoma/leukaemia) dumps cell contents into the blood → AKI + dangerous electrolytes.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — TLS derangements: 'high-high-high-LOW' — uric acid, K, PO₄ up; Ca down**\n\nHIGH uric acid (nucleic-acid breakdown) → precipitates in tubules → AKI.\n\nHIGH potassium (arrhythmia risk).\n\nHIGH phosphate → binds calcium → calcium-phosphate precipitates in tubules (more AKI).\n\nLOW calcium (sucked into those complexes) → tetany/arrhythmia.\n\n**🔑  Prevention/treatment**\n\nAggressive IV HYDRATION (flush the tubules) + close electrolyte monitoring.\n\nALLOPURINOL (blocks new uric acid formation) — prophylaxis.\n\nRASBURICASE (recombinant uricase — actually breaks DOWN existing uric acid) — for high-risk/established TLS.\n\nDialysis if severe (refractory hyperkalaemia, AKI, severe acidosis).\n\n## **Minimal Change Disease (MCD)**\n\n**What it is  **The commonest nephrotic syndrome in CHILDREN (~90%). 'Minimal change' = the glomerulus looks NORMAL on light microscopy.\n\n**🔑  Mechanism**\n\nT cells release a circulating permeability factor → it neutralises the negative charge on the GBM + fuses podocyte foot processes → selective loss of albumin (charge barrier lost) → nephrotic syndrome.\n\nOften triggered by a recent viral infection or vaccination. Secondary: NSAIDs, Hodgkin lymphoma.\n\n**Presentation  **Sudden-onset nephrotic syndrome: massive (selective, mostly albumin) proteinuria, periorbital oedema/anasarca, NORMAL BP, normal kidney function initially.\n\n**Diagnosis  **LM normal, IF normal, serology normal — the diagnosis is on ELECTRON microscopy (podocyte foot-process effacement). In children you usually skip biopsy and trial steroids; in adults you biopsy.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — MCD = 'Minimal on microscopy, Massive response to steroids'**\n\n~90% respond to steroids, no progression to CKD, but can relapse (re-treat). If it does NOT respond → re-biopsy and suspect FSGS (which MCD can be mistaken for / progress to).\n\n## **Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)**\n\n**What it is  **Scarring of SOME glomeruli (focal), in PART of each affected glomerulus (segmental). A major nephrotic cause, esp. in African-American and older patients — poorer prognosis.\n\n**🔑  Mechanism + causes**\n\nPrimary (idiopathic): a circulating permeability factor binds podocyte receptors → cytoskeletal disorganisation → podocytes detach → plasma leaks into Bowman's space → scarring.\n\nSecondary: anything causing hyperfiltration/stress — HIV, obesity, heroin, reduced nephron mass, sickle cell, reflux. Genetic: podocyte protein mutations (nephrin/podocin).\n\n**Presentation  **Nephrotic syndrome OFTEN WITH hypertension + microscopic haematuria (vs MCD which has normal BP and no haematuria).\n\n**Diagnosis / treatment  **LM: segmental sclerosis/hyalinosis; EM: podocyte effacement; IF unremarkable. Less steroid-responsive than MCD → add calcineurin inhibitors. ~50% reach ESRD within 10 years. In PRIMARY FSGS, transplanted kidneys can be re-attacked (the circulating factor) → plasmapheresis.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — MCD vs FSGS at a glance**\n\nMCD: child, normal BP, no haematuria, steroid-responsive, no scarring.\n\nFSGS: adult/African-American, hypertension + microhaematuria, steroid-resistant, segmental scarring, often → ESRD.\n\n## **Membranoproliferative GN (MPGN)**\n\n**What it is  **A glomerular disease with mesangial proliferation + GBM 'double-contour' (tram-track) appearance. Presents as a MIXED nephritic + nephrotic picture — and classically LOW C3.\n\n**🔑  The two mechanisms**\n\nType 1 (subendothelial immune-complex): chronic antigenaemia (HBV/HCV, monoclonal gammopathy, chronic infection) → immune complexes deposit subendothelially → complement activation.\n\nType 2 (Dense Deposit Disease): C3 nephritic factor (an autoantibody) stabilises C3 convertase → relentless complement activation → C3 in the GBM.\n\nEither way: complement injures podocytes/endothelium + drives mesangial cells to grow between old and new GBM = TRAM-TRACK.\n\n**Diagnosis  **Serology: LOW C3 (± low C4 in type 1), C3 nephritic factor positive in type 2. LM: tram-tracking. Mixed nephritic-nephrotic clinically.\n\n**Treatment  **Treat underlying cause (e.g. antivirals for HCV); steroids/immunosuppression have limited efficacy.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — MPGN = 'tram-tracks + low C3 + mixed picture'**\n\nDouble-contour ('tram-track') GBM, persistently LOW complement (C3), and it straddles nephritic AND nephrotic. Always look for hepatitis C.\n\n## **Lupus Nephritis**\n\n**What it is  **Kidney involvement in SLE — immune-complex glomerulonephritis. Classified ISN/RPS Class I–VI; class drives treatment.\n\n| **Class** | **Lesion** | **Picture** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **I / II** | Minimal / mesangial | Mild urinary findings |\n| **III / IV** | Focal / DIFFUSE proliferative (IV = most severe & common) | Nephritic ± nephrotic, AKI, active sediment — needs aggressive treatment |\n| **V** | Membranous | Nephrotic syndrome |\n| **VI** | Advanced sclerosis | Scarred, ESRD |\n\n**Diagnosis  **ANA positive (~95%, sensitive), anti-dsDNA (specific, tracks activity), LOW complement (C3/C4). KIDNEY BIOPSY determines the class → guides treatment. LM of class IV: 'wire-loop' capillaries.\n\n**Treatment (class III/IV)  **Induction with steroids + mycophenolate or cyclophosphamide; maintenance with mycophenolate or azathioprine.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Lupus can be BOTH nephritic and nephrotic**\n\nClass IV (diffuse proliferative) = severe NEPHRITIC (wire-loops, low C3, anti-dsDNA). Class V = NEPHROTIC (membranous pattern). Biopsy class decides the drugs.\n\n## **ANCA-Associated Vasculitides (GPA · MPA · EGPA)**\n\n**What they are  **Small-vessel vasculitides that cause PAUCI-IMMUNE RPGN (crescents, NO immune deposits on IF). Driven by ANCA autoantibodies against neutrophils → neutrophils degranulate → vessel wall necrosis.\n\n| **Disease** | **ANCA** | **Extra-renal clues** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **GPA (granulomatosis with polyangiitis)** | c-ANCA (anti-PR3), ~90% | Upper airway (sinusitis, SADDLE NOSE) + lung nodules/cavities + GN. GRANULOMAS present. |\n| **MPA (microscopic polyangiitis)** | p-ANCA (anti-MPO), ~60% | Lung capillaritis (haemoptysis) + GN. NO granulomas. |\n| **EGPA (eosinophilic GPA / Churg-Strauss)** | p-ANCA (anti-MPO), ~40% | ASTHMA + EOSINOPHILIA + GN. Granulomas + eosinophils. |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Tell them apart by the EXTRA organ**\n\nGPA = c-ANCA, upper-airway granulomas + SADDLE NOSE ('GPA = Granulomas + PR3').\n\nMPA = p-ANCA, lungs + kidney, NO granulomas ('Microscopic = no granulomas').\n\nEGPA = p-ANCA, ASTHMA + EOSINOPHILS ('E for Eosinophils/asthma').\n\n### **Renal presentation (common to all three)**\n\n- RPGN: rapidly rising creatinine + haematuria + RBC casts. Biopsy: crescents (>50%), necrotising GN, PAUCI-IMMUNE on IF.\n\n**Treatment  **Urgent steroids + cyclophosphamide or rituximab ± plasma exchange (severe). Like all RPGN — speed prevents permanent loss.\n\n## **Alport Syndrome**\n\n**What it is  **A hereditary type-IV collagen defect → the basement membrane of KIDNEY, EARS and EYES is faulty. Mostly X-linked (COL4A5); also autosomal recessive (COL4A3/4).\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Alport TRIAD = Kidney + Ear + Eye**\n\nNEPHRITIC syndrome (haematuria → progressive GN → ESRD, males by 20s–30s) + SENSORINEURAL HEARING LOSS + EYE changes (anterior LENTICONUS / cataracts).\n\nNormal C3 + deafness + eye signs = Alport (separates it from the low-C3 and IgA causes).\n\n**Diagnosis/treatment  **EM: GBM thinning + 'basket-weave' splitting of the lamina densa. ACEi/ARB for proteinuria; dialysis/transplant for failure (rarely, post-transplant anti-GBM/Goodpasture can develop because the new collagen is 'foreign').\n\n## **Fabry Disease (brief)**\n\n**What it is  **X-linked lysosomal storage disease (alpha-galactosidase A deficiency) → globotriaosylceramide accumulates → renal failure + neuropathic pain + angiokeratomas + cardiac/cerebrovascular disease. Treated with enzyme replacement. Know it exists as a genetic cause of CKD.\n\n# **PART 10 · ELECTROLYTES ****&**** ACID-BASE — DEEP REFERENCE**\n\nHyponatraemia/hypernatraemia and hyperkalaemia are covered in Part 5. Here is the rest of the electrolyte/acid-base content from the notes, kept tight.\n\n## **Hypokalaemia (K⁺ ****<**** 3.5)**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — HypoK = 'Shifted IN, or Lost (gut/kidney), or low Mg'**\n\nSHIFT into cells: insulin, beta-2 agonists (salbutamol), alkalosis.\n\nGI loss: vomiting (→ also metabolic alkalosis) or diarrhoea (→ also metabolic acidosis) — the acid/base direction tells you which.\n\nRENAL loss: loop/thiazide diuretics, RTA types 1 & 2, hyperaldosteronism, low magnesium (check Mg in refractory cases!).\n\n### **ECG + effects**\n\n- ECG: T-wave FLATTENING → inversion → ST depression → prominent U WAVE. Long QT → risk of Torsade (worse if Mg also low).\n\n- Muscle: weakness; cardiac: increased excitability/arrhythmia.\n\n**🚩  EXAM TRAP — Refractory hypokalaemia = check magnesium**\n\nLow Mg makes the kidney waste K and stops you correcting it. You CANNOT fix the K until you fix the Mg. Classic exam trap.\n\n## **Acid-Base Disorders — the framework**\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — Step 1 pH → Step 2 primary driver → Step 3 anion gap → Step 4 compensation**\n\npH <7.35 = acidosis, >7.45 = alkalosis.\n\nLook at CO₂ (respiratory) and HCO₃ (metabolic) to find the PRIMARY problem.\n\nFor metabolic acidosis, compute the ANION GAP = Na − (Cl + HCO₃); normal 4–12.\n\nCheck whether compensation is appropriate (e.g. Winter's formula for metabolic acidosis).\n\n### **Metabolic acidosis**\n\n| **Anion gap** | **Mechanism** | **Causes** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **HIGH (****>****12)** | Added acid (unmeasured anion) | Diabetic ketoacidosis, uraemia (AKI/ESRD), lactic acidosis (shock; metformin), toxic alcohols (methanol/ethylene glycol) |\n| **NORMAL** | Bicarbonate lost / H⁺ not excreted | Diarrhoea (GI bicarb loss), RTA (types 1/2/4), mild CKD |\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — High-anion-gap causes: 'MUDPILES'-style → DKA, Uraemia, Lactate, Toxins**\n\nThe big four to name fast: ketoacidosis, uraemia, lactic acidosis, toxic alcohols. Normal-gap = diarrhoea or RTA.\n\n### **Metabolic alkalosis**\n\n- Renal H⁺ loss: loop/thiazide diuretics (hypovolaemic), hyperaldosteronism (hypervolaemic/hypertensive). GI H⁺ loss: VOMITING.\n\n- Complications: hypokalaemia, compensatory hypoventilation, low ionised calcium → tetany.\n\n### **Respiratory acidosis vs alkalosis**\n\n|  | **Respiratory ACIDOSIS (↑CO₂)** | **Respiratory ALKALOSIS (↓CO₂)** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Cause** | HYPOventilation: opioids/benzos, stroke, neuromuscular disease, COPD/asthma airway obstruction | HYPERventilation: pain/anxiety, hypoxaemia (altitude, PE, pneumonia, ARDS), aspirin toxicity |\n| **Brain effect** | ↑CO₂ → cerebral vasoDILATION → ↑ICP → lethargy | ↓CO₂ → cerebral vasoCONSTRICTION → ↓ICP → syncope (therapeutic in ↑ICP) |\n\n**Compensation timing  **Acute respiratory acidosis: HCO₃ rises 1 per 10 mmHg ↑CO₂ (cell buffering). Chronic (>24h): HCO₃ rises 4 per 10 mmHg (renal adaptation).\n\n## **Dialysis ****&**** Transplant — essentials**\n\n### **Haemodialysis**\n\n- Needs an ARTERIOVENOUS FISTULA (artery joined to superficial vein → vein 'arterialises', tough enough for big needles + high flow). Takes ~4 weeks to mature; use a central venous catheter if urgent (less efficient, more infection).\n\n- Blood + dialysate flow on opposite sides of a semipermeable membrane → solutes move down their gradient. Heparin prevents clotting in the circuit.\n\n### **Peritoneal dialysis**\n\n- Uses the patient's PERITONEUM as the membrane: 2 L dialysate in via a catheter, dwell ~5–6h, drain, repeat (3–4×/day).\n\n- Glucose in the dialysate creates the osmotic pull (higher glucose = more fluid removed). CONTRAINDICATED relative concern in diabetics (glucose absorption).\n\n- Best for hypotensive patients + those wanting home/independent dialysis; preserves residual function better; but higher PERITONITIS risk.\n\n**🧠  MEMORY TRICK — HD vs PD**\n\nHaemodialysis = machine + fistula, 3×/week at a centre, risk = access infection/hypotension. Peritoneal = home, daily, uses your own belly lining, risk = peritonitis + glucose load (bad for diabetics).\n\n### **Transplant**\n\n- Best therapy for ESRD. First-year death risk is DOUBLE dialysis (surgery + immunosuppression infection + steroid CV risk), then far lower. Best done pre-emptively at GFR 20–25.\n\n- Donor priority: living pre-emptive > living > deceased > living-who-did-dialysis. Living kidneys: shorter ischaemia, known history, shorter wait.\n\n- Native kidneys left in place EXCEPT in PKD (too big — see Q40). Immunosuppression stages: desensitisation → induction → maintenance → anti-rejection. Malignancy is the 2nd commonest cause of death (immunosuppression) → quarterly cancer screening; steroids can cause new-onset diabetes.\n\n# **PART 11 · KNOW-IT-EXISTS APPENDIX (rarely/not examined)**\n\nGenuinely low-yield for your oral — one line each so you recognise the name if it comes up, no need to memorise detail.\n\n**📎  Quick recognitions**\n\nBartter syndrome — genetic loop-of-Henle defect (like being on a loop diuretic): hypokalaemia + metabolic alkalosis, normal/low BP.\n\nGitelman syndrome — genetic DCT defect (like a thiazide): hypokalaemia + alkalosis + LOW magnesium + low urinary calcium.\n\nLiddle syndrome — overactive ENaC: hypertension + hypokalaemia with LOW aldosterone.\n\nAtypical HUS — complement dysregulation → thrombotic microangiopathy (AKI + low platelets + haemolysis).\n\nNephrogenic systemic fibrosis — from gadolinium in advanced CKD (see contrast rules).\n\nSpecific antibiotic/immunosuppressant dosing — not usually tested in oral exams; know that doses are adjusted to GFR and that aminoglycosides/vancomycin are nephrotoxic.\n\n# **⚡ QUICK REFERENCE — NUMBERS ****&**** ONE-LINERS**\n\n## **Numbers worth memorising**\n\n| **Item** | **Value** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **CKD definition** | GFR <60 OR kidney damage, for ≥3 months |\n| **Kidney-failure threshold** | GFR <15 (Stage 5 / ESRD) |\n| **The 'magic' CKD number** | 45 (Stage 3a→3b = endocrine function fails; start EPO + bone Rx) |\n| **Normal GFR / filtered volume** | ~120 mL/min / ~180 L/day |\n| **Nephrotic proteinuria** | > 3.5 g/day |\n| **Nephritic proteinuria** | < 2.5 g/day (mild) + haematuria/RBC casts |\n| **Albumin at which oedema starts** | < 3 g/dL (severe < 2.5) |\n| **Microalbuminuria** | 30–300 mg/day (earliest diabetic sign) |\n| **RBCs to call nephritic** | 1 RBC cast OR 1–5 dysmorphic/acanthocytes per HPF |\n| **Anaemia (men/women)** | Hb <13 / <12 g/dL; renal anaemia appears at Stage 3b |\n| **Renal anaemia Hb target** | 11–12.5 g/dL (NOT normal — avoid thrombosis) |\n| **Normal PTH** | 15–65 pg/mL |\n| **Hyperkalaemia** | K⁺ > 5.3 mEq/L |\n| **'Rule of 7' emergency** | pH <7 + K⁺ >7 + PO₄ >7 |\n| **AKI 'acute' window** | onset 2–48 h; abrupt ↓urine + ↑creatinine |\n| **AKI causes split** | Pre-renal 70% · post-renal 20% · intra-renal 10% |\n| **Oliguria / anuria / polyuria** | <400 / <100 / >2.5–3 L per day |\n| **Avoid iodinated contrast / gadolinium** | GFR <60 (iodine) / GFR <30 (gadolinium) |\n| **Normal creatinine (M/F)** | 0.7–1.2 / 0.5–1.0 mg/dL |\n\n## **Pattern one-liners**\n\n| **If you see…** | **Think…** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Cola urine + dysmorphic RBCs + RBC casts** | Glomerular (nephritic) |\n| **Bright red urine + normal RBCs + no casts** | Urological (stone/infection/cancer) |\n| **Muddy-brown granular casts** | Acute tubular necrosis |\n| **WBC casts + fever + flank pain + Giordano +** | Pyelonephritis |\n| **WBC casts + rash + eosinophils + new drug** | Acute interstitial nephritis |\n| **Fatty casts + ****>****3.5 g protein + low albumin** | Nephrotic syndrome |\n| **Sore throat THEN haematuria same day** | IgA nephropathy |\n| **Sore throat then haematuria 2 WEEKS later, low C3** | Post-streptococcal GN |\n| **Haemoptysis + cola urine + rising creatinine** | Goodpasture (anti-GBM) |\n| **Purpura + nephritic + abdo pain in a teen** | IgA vasculitis (HSP) |\n| **Saddle nose + lung cavities + c-ANCA** | GPA |\n| **Asthma + eosinophilia + p-ANCA** | EGPA |\n| **Nephrotic + anti-PLA2R (adult)** | Membranous nephropathy |\n| **Nephrotic child, normal microscopy** | Minimal change disease |\n| **Deafness + eye lenticonus + haematuria** | Alport syndrome |\n| **Tram-track GBM + low C3 + mixed picture** | MPGN |\n| **Resistant HTN + creatinine spikes on ACEi** | Renal artery stenosis |\n| **Huge kidneys + berry aneurysm + liver cysts** | Polycystic kidney disease |\n| **Cirrhosis + rising creatinine + low urine Na** | Hepatorenal syndrome |\n| **Crush/seizure + CK****>****5000 + dipstick-blood, no RBCs** | Rhabdomyolysis |\n| **Post-chemo + high uric acid/K/PO₄ + low Ca** | Tumour lysis syndrome |\n\n## **Final memory hooks**\n\n- Nephritic = bleeds (blood + casts + HTN). Nephrotic = leaks protein (>3.5g + low albumin + lipids + periorbital oedema).\n\n- Child nephrotic = Minimal change (steroids). Adult nephrotic = Membranous (anti-PLA2R).\n\n- RPGN by immunofluorescence: Linear = anti-GBM · Lumpy = immune complex · Lacking = ANCA.\n\n- AKI location: Pre (70%, perfusion) · Intra (10%, ATN) · Post (20%, obstruction). US first.\n\n- CKD complications = HAM-U BEEP. The Rule of 7 (pH/K/PO₄ all >7-ish) = emergency dialysis.\n\n- Hyperkalaemia ECG: Peak T → lose P → wide QRS → sine → arrest. Calcium protects, insulin/salbutamol shift, resin/dialysis remove.\n\n- 45 = the magic GFR (endocrine failure starts). 15 = failure. <30 no gadolinium, <60 no iodine contrast."}};
